window.__NOTE_CONTENT = "<p>The Law of Insurance</p>\n<h2 id=\"insurance-law\">INSURANCE LAW</h2>\n<h4 id=\"formation-of-insurance-contracts\">Formation of Insurance Contracts:</h4>\n<h4 id=\"topic-1\">Topic 1:</h4>\n<h3 id=\"1-explain-the-meaning-of-insurance\">1. Explain the meaning of Insurance</h3>\n<p>Read Department of Trade and Industry v St. Christopher</p>\n<p>Motorists Association [1974] 1 All ER 395 on the challenge of</p>\n<p>formulating a legal definition of insurance.</p>\n<p>Also see Medical Defence Union v Department of Trade [1979]</p>\n<p>2 All ER 421 at 429</p>\n<p>“A contract for insurance is any contract whereby one party assumes</p>\n<p>the risk of an uncertain event, which is not within his control,</p>\n<p>happening at a future time, in which the other party has an interest, and</p>\n<p>under which contract the first party is bound to pay money or provide</p>\n<p>its equivalent if the uncertain event occurs.” – John Birds, Modern Law</p>\n<p>of Insurance at pg. 11.</p>\n<p>In   Prudential       Insurance       Co.      v.     Inland    Revenue</p>\n<p>Commissioners [1904] 2 K. B. 658 at 664, Channell J stated that “a</p>\n<p>contract of insurance must be a contract for the payment of a sum of</p>\n<p>money, or for some corresponding benefit to become due on the</p>\n<p>happening of an event, which event must be of a character more or less</p>\n<p>adverse to the interest of the person effecting the insurance.”</p>\n<p>In this case, a one W. R. Aucutt took out a policy for W. Aucutt</p>\n<p>his son: the company agreed that in consideration for a weekly</p>\n<p>premium to be paid to them by the assured until he should turn</p>\n<p>65, the company would pay 95/ and if he died before that age,</p>\n<p>they would pay to his executors or administrators the sum of 30/.</p>\n<p>The instrument provided that after it had been in force for five</p>\n<p>years, the company would, in exchange for the same, grant certain</p>\n<p>pecuniary benefits specified in a schedule thereto –</p>\n<p>corresponding with the age of the assured and the duration of the</p>\n<p>policy.</p>\n<p>The case explains the difference between life insurance policies</p>\n<p>and endowment assurances: “Many kinds of</p>\n<p>instruments purporting to be policies of assurance are now</p>\n<p>issued by life assurance companies under which sums are</p>\n<p>contracted to be paid, either (a) at a particular period or on</p>\n<p>the previous death of the assured or (b) on the assured</p>\n<p>attaining a particular age or on the previous death of the</p>\n<p>assured (in each of these cases, where the same amount of</p>\n<p>money is payable in either event, according to the</p>\n<p>practice of the Commissioners life policy duty is charged);</p>\n<p>and in the case of some instruments sums are thereby</p>\n<p>contracted to be paid at the expiration of a particular period, if</p>\n<p>the assured is living, and a further sum at death.”</p>\n<p>The question to be determined was whether the instrument in this</p>\n<p>case was a policy of insurance upon any life or upon any event or</p>\n<p>contingency relating to or depending upon any life or lives.</p>\n<p>“It must be a contract whereby for some consideration, usually</p>\n<p>but not necessarily for periodical payments called premiums, you</p>\n<p>secure to yourself some benefit, usually but not necessarily the</p>\n<p>payment of a sum of money, upon the happening of some event.</p>\n<p>Then the next thing that is necessary is that the event should be</p>\n<p>one which involves some amount of uncertainty. There must be</p>\n<p>either uncertainty whether the event will ever happen or not, or if</p>\n<p>the event is one which must happen at some time there must be</p>\n<p>uncertainty as to the time at which it will happen. The remaining</p>\n<p>essential is that which was referred to by the Attorney-General</p>\n<p>when he said the insurance must be against something. A contract</p>\n<p>which would otherwise be a mere wager may become an</p>\n<p>insurance by reason of the assured having an interest in the</p>\n<p>subject-matter - that is to say, the uncertain event which is</p>\n<p>necessary to make the contract amount to an insurance must be an</p>\n<p>event which is prima facie adverse to the interest of the assured.”</p>\n<p>The insurance is to provide for the payment of a sum of money to</p>\n<p>meet a loss or detriment which will or may be suffered upon the</p>\n<p>happening of the event.</p>\n<p>Court found that in this case, even if the endowment portion of</p>\n<p>the policy stood alone – and if the contract purported to be</p>\n<p>nothing more than a provision against old age, it would still be a</p>\n<p>policy of insurance and if a policy of insurance, then also a policy</p>\n<p>of life insurance – as it would be an insurance upon a contingency</p>\n<p>relating to life.</p>\n<p>Life Insurance: &quot;The contract commonly called &#x27;life assurance,&#x27;</p>\n<p>when properly considered, is a mere contract to pay a certain sum of</p>\n<p>money on the death of a person, in consideration of the due payment</p>\n<p>of a certain annuity for his life, - the amount of the annuity being</p>\n<p>calculated, in the first instance, according to the probable duration of</p>\n<p>the life; and when once fixed it is constant and invariable.&quot; - Dalby</p>\n<p>v. India and London Life Assurance Co. (1854) 15 C. B. 365,</p>\n<p>387.</p>\n<p>“A contract of life insurance is one by which persons entitle their</p>\n<p>executors to receive a sum of money for distribution among their</p>\n<p>family in the event of their death.” – Prudential Insurance Co. v.</p>\n<p>Inland Revenue Commissioners [1904] 2 K. B. 658 at 664.</p>\n<p>Carter v Boehm (1766) 3 Burr 1905: The action was on a policy of</p>\n<p>insurance for one year, viz, from 16 October 1759, to 16 October 1760,</p>\n<p>for the benefit of the governor of Fort Marlborough – George Carter,</p>\n<p>against the loss of Fort Marlborough in the island of Sumatra in the</p>\n<p>East Indies, by its being taken by foreign enemy. The fort was taken in</p>\n<p>fact by the French within the year. The first witness was Cawthorne, the</p>\n<p>policy-broker, who produced the memorandum given by the plaintiff to</p>\n<p>him and the use made of these instructions to show that the insurance</p>\n<p>was made &quot;for the benefit of Governor Carter, and to insure him</p>\n<p>against the taking of the fort by a foreign enemy.</p>\n<p>The defendants contended that the plaintiff was guilty of fraud –</p>\n<p>because he concealed circumstances which ought to have been disclosed</p>\n<p>i.e., the weakness of the Fort and the probability that it would be</p>\n<p>attacked by the French.</p>\n<p>Lord Mansfield examined concealments that avoid a policy: “…</p>\n<p>Insurance is a contract upon speculation. The special facts</p>\n<p>upon which the contingent chance is to be computed lie most</p>\n<p>commonly in the knowledge of the insured only. The</p>\n<p>underwriter trusts to his representation and proceeds upon the</p>\n<p>confidence that he does not keep back any circumstance in his</p>\n<p>knowledge to misled the underwriter into a belief that the</p>\n<p>circumstance does not exist, and to induce him to estimate the</p>\n<p>risk as if it did not exist. Keeping back such a circumstance is a</p>\n<p>fraud – and therefore the policy is void. Although the suppression</p>\n<p>should happen through mistake without any fraudulent intention, yet</p>\n<p>still the underwriter is deceived, and the policy is void, because the</p>\n<p>risk run is really different from the risk understood and intended to</p>\n<p>be run at the time of the agreement. The policy would equally be</p>\n<p>void, against the underwriter if he concealed, as if he insured a ship</p>\n<p>on her voyage which he privately knew to be arrived, and an action</p>\n<p>would lie to recover the premium.”</p>\n<p>“Good faith forbids either party, by concealing what he privately knows,</p>\n<p>to draw the other into a bargain from his ignorance of that fact and his</p>\n<p>believing the contrary. But either party may be innocently silent, as to</p>\n<p>grounds open to both, to exercise their judgment upon. Aliud est celare;</p>\n<p>aliud, tacere; neque enim id est celare quiequid reticeas; sed cum quod</p>\n<p>tuscias, id ignorare emolumenti tui causa velis eos, quorum intersit id</p>\n<p>scire: CICERO, DE OFF, lib 3, c 12, 13. This definition of</p>\n<p>concealment, restrained to the efficient motives and precise subject of</p>\n<p>any contract, will generally hold to make it void in favour of the party</p>\n<p>misled by his ignorance of the thing concealed.”</p>\n<p>Lord Mansfield noted, however, that there are matters as to which the</p>\n<p>insured may be innocently silent – he need not mention what the</p>\n<p>underwriter knows and an underwriter cannot insist that the policy is</p>\n<p>void because the insured did not tell him what he actually knew.</p>\n<p>“The reason of the rule which obliges parties to disclose is to prevent</p>\n<p>fraud and to encourage good faith. It is adapted to such facts as vary the</p>\n<p>nature of the contract which one privately knows, and the other is</p>\n<p>ignorant of and has no reason to suspect. The question, therefore, must</p>\n<p>always be whether there was, under all the circumstances at the time the</p>\n<p>policy was underwritten, a fair representation or a concealment,</p>\n<p>fraudulent if designed, or, though</p>\n<p>not designed, varying materially the object of the policy, and changing</p>\n<p>the risk understood to be run.”</p>\n<p>In the instant case, court found that the condition, state and strength</p>\n<p>Fort Marlborough was ‘in general well known by most persons</p>\n<p>conversant or acquainted with Indian affairs or the state of the</p>\n<p>company&#x27;s factories or settlements, and could not be kept secret or</p>\n<p>concealed from persons who should endeavor by proper inquiry to</p>\n<p>inform themselves.’ There would not be a re-trial.</p>\n<p>Aqua Plumbing (U) Limited v United Assurance Company</p>\n<p>Limited, Civil Suit 431 of 2002: The plaintiff sued the defendant for</p>\n<p>breach of insurance contract for a vehicle that got into an accident, and</p>\n<p>prayed for compensation, general damages with interest and costs. The</p>\n<p>defendant, on the other hand, denied that there was ever an insurance</p>\n<p>policy and prayed that the court dismiss the court.</p>\n<p>The issue in the case was whether at the time of the accident, the vehicle</p>\n<p>had comprehensive motor vehicle insurance covering it (and of course,</p>\n<p>remedies).</p>\n<p>The plaintiff had taken out a policy from 14th March 2000 to 13th March</p>\n<p>2001 – paying a premium of 658,000/= and on 13th March, 2001 – the</p>\n<p>defendant sent the plaintiff a renewal notice. The plaintiff responded</p>\n<p>and the defendant sent him a third-party insurance receipt showing that</p>\n<p>the policy could be used to cover the vehicle comprehensively,</p>\n<p>because it cited the plaintiff’s initial comprehensive insurance policy and</p>\n<p>the old premium was payable. The plaintiff, however, did not pay the</p>\n<p>premium for the renewal of the policy for its vehicle until May 9th 2002.</p>\n<p>In the meantime, the vehicle had a serious accident and was written off.</p>\n<p>The plaintiff sought to recover the pre-accident value of the vehicle –</p>\n<p>which the defendant refused to compensate.</p>\n<p>Court differentiated between a comprehensive motor vehicle insurance</p>\n<h4 id=\"policy\">policy:</h4>\n<p>i.    A comprehensive motor vehicle insurance policy” as the</p>\n<p>name suggests is a motor vehicle insurance policy that covers</p>\n<p>the insured in respect of a wide range of risks his/her motor</p>\n<p>vehicle may face as it plies the roads. For</p>\n<p>example, the motor vehicle in question may be damaged on the</p>\n<p>road. It may cause damage to other motor vehicles or property</p>\n<p>as it runs on the road. It may cause death or personal injuries to</p>\n<p>individuals, etc. A comprehensive motor insurance policy</p>\n<p>covers the insured against all such risks; and the insurer is</p>\n<p>under obligation to indemnify the insured when they occur.</p>\n<p>ii.   ‘A Third-Party motor vehicle insurance policy” (in Uganda)</p>\n<p>covers the insured only in respect of death or personal injury</p>\n<p>occasioned to a third party as a result of an accident which the</p>\n<p>motor vehicle of the insured causes. It does not cover the</p>\n<p>insured in respect of damage to his/her motor vehicle sustained</p>\n<p>on the road or, damage his/her motor vehicle may cause to any</p>\n<p>other motor vehicle or property – section 3(b) of the Motor</p>\n<p>Vehicle Insurance (Third Party Risks) Act, Cap 214.</p>\n<p>Although these policies are different, generally when one takes out</p>\n<p>comprehensive motor insurance policy, it means that one’s insurance</p>\n<p>cover includes a third-party motor vehicle insurance policy. Under</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-2-1-of-the-motor-vehicle-insurance-third-party\">section 2(1) of the Motor Vehicle Insurance (Third Party</h3>\n<p>Risks) Act (Cap. 214), all motor vehicle owners are under a legal</p>\n<p>obligation to take out a Third Party motor vehicle insurance policy if</p>\n<p>they are to use their motor vehicles lawfully on the roads. However,</p>\n<p>those motor vehicle owners are under no legal obligation to take out</p>\n<p>a comprehensive motor insurance policy in respect of their motor</p>\n<p>vehicles. The effect of this, therefore, is that many motor vehicle</p>\n<p>owners fulfill their legal obligation by taking out a Third Party motor</p>\n<p>vehicle insurance policy in respect of their motor vehicles, but they</p>\n<p>deliberately avoid obtaining comprehensive cover for their motor</p>\n<p>vehicles because it is expensive to do so.</p>\n<p>“Where the insurer and the insured have agreed that the insurer</p>\n<p>would cover a given risk for a specific period, there exists a contract</p>\n<p>of insurance between the two parties for the period agreed upon.</p>\n<p>Where the agreed period of cover expires and the contract insurance</p>\n<p>is not renewed, it is obvious that the validity of such contract</p>\n<p>ceases – Brown v Graham (1963) SR NSW</p>\n<h3 id=\"365-however-where-the-contract-is-renewed-the-renewal-becomes-a\">365. However, where the contract is renewed, the renewal becomes a</h3>\n<p>fresh contract of insurance - Hanley v Pacific Fire &amp;</p>\n<p>Marine Insurance Company (1893) 14 LR (NSW) 224; Re</p>\n<p>Kerr (1943) SASR 8; and Stockell v Heywood (1897) 1.</p>\n<p>Ch. 459).</p>\n<p>“The new contract begins from the day of renewal and runs until the</p>\n<p>agreed day of expiry. It follows, therefore, that the risk occurring in</p>\n<p>the interim period, that is to say between the expiry of the policy and</p>\n<p>the renewal thereof is not covered. To purport to say that such risk</p>\n<p>was covered under the renewed policy would, in Court’s opinion, go</p>\n<p>against the basic principle of insurance, which is to cover an</p>\n<p>uncertain future risk and not a past event.” In this case, the plaintiff</p>\n<p>could not make a claim basing on a policy that was not in existence at</p>\n<p>the time of the accident. Suit dismissed.</p>\n<h4 id=\"requirements-for-existence-of-a-valid-insurance-contract\">Requirements for existence of a valid insurance contract:</h4>\n<p>a. A binding contract: the insurer must be legally bound to</p>\n<p>compensate the other party.</p>\n<p>It is not enough that there is a right to be considered for a benefit</p>\n<p>– Medical Defence Union v Department of Trade: the</p>\n<p>plaintiff was a company whose members were practicing doctors</p>\n<p>and dentists. Its business primarily consisted of conducting legal</p>\n<p>proceedings against claims made against members for damages</p>\n<p>and costs.</p>\n<p>However, under the plaintiff’s constitution, the members had no</p>\n<p>right to such benefits, merely the right to request for assistance or</p>\n<p>an indemnity.</p>\n<p>It was held that the company was not carrying on insurance</p>\n<p>business. To be a contract of insurance, a contract must provide</p>\n<p>for the right of the insured to money, or money’s worth on the</p>\n<p>happening of the uncertain event. The right to request for</p>\n<p>assistance was no such right.</p>\n<p>b. Uncertainty: this is a necessary feature of insurance and</p>\n<p>lies as to whether the event insured against will occur. In life</p>\n<p>insurance, it is as to the time when it will occur.</p>\n<p>Read Gould v Curtis [1913] 3 KB 84. (This case discusses</p>\n<p>life insurance and endowment policies)</p>\n<p>The respondent held a policy under which an insurance</p>\n<p>company covenanted with him that if he should die before</p>\n<p>March 1, 1923, they will pay 100l. on his death, or if he</p>\n<p>should be living on that day they would at that date pay him</p>\n<p>200l. The annual premium was 11l. 11s. 8d. The question</p>\n<p>was whether he was entitled to have an allowance by way of</p>\n<p>deduction of that annual premium under s. 54 of the</p>\n<p>Income Tax Act, 1853.</p>\n<p>In this case, it was argued that the contract in dispute was</p>\n<p>not a contract of insurance because life is not an event that</p>\n<p>can fairly be said to be adverse to the interests of the</p>\n<p>insurer. Court held that it is not true to say that there can be</p>\n<p>no insurance except the event covered by the contract is one</p>\n<p>which is in its nature adverse to the assured.</p>\n<p>Buckley, LJ on Uncertainty: “I wish to add a word as to</p>\n<p>something which was said by Channell J. in the case of</p>\n<p>Prudential      Insurance Co.         v.    Inland     Revenue</p>\n<p>Commissioners. (1) The particular words to which I</p>\n<p>venture to take exception are these, at p. 664: &quot;which</p>\n<p>event must have some amount of uncertainty about it,</p>\n<p>and must be of a character more or less adverse to the</p>\n<p>interest of the person effecting the insurance.&quot; The</p>\n<p>learned judge was using those words in connection with a</p>\n<p>policy of life insurance, and he gave a reason there why</p>\n<p>he thought there was in that case an adverse risk.” “If the</p>\n<p>policy be one such as a fire policy, or a marine</p>\n<p>policy upon         a vessel, it          is a policy        of</p>\n<p>indemnity,       an     obligation      to indemnify.          In</p>\n<p>insurances of that class, I agree that what you look at</p>\n<p>is to see whether there has occurred an event</p>\n<p>adverse to the person who is insured, such as that,</p>\n<p>having suffered a loss by reason of that adverse</p>\n<p>event, he is to be indemnified by the sum which is</p>\n<p>guaranteed to him under the policy. The same is not</p>\n<p>true of a policy of life       insurance. A policy of        life</p>\n<p>insurance is not a policy of indemnity, but is a policy</p>\n<p>upon a contingency. Death cannot for this purpose be</p>\n<p>appropriately described as an adverse event. It is an</p>\n<p>event which some people regard as adverse, and some</p>\n<p>do not. But for this particular purpose it is not adverse, for it</p>\n<p>is not in the sense that it occasions pecuniary loss, which is</p>\n<p>what is meant in the case of a fire or marine policy. The</p>\n<p>obligation in a policy of life insurance is not based upon any</p>\n<p>doctrine of compensating the person for the event. Money is</p>\n<p>no compensation for death. An insurance upon life is the</p>\n<p>creation in favour of a person who has an insurable interest</p>\n<p>of an obligation to pay money in an event, namely, the</p>\n<p>contingency of human life. Whether the contingency be the</p>\n<p>continuance of life at a date or whether it be death, in</p>\n<p>both cases it seems to me that it is included within the</p>\n<p>expression &quot;insurance on life&quot; contained in s. 54.”</p>\n<p>c. Insurable Interest: the insured must have an insurable</p>\n<p>interest in the property or life or liability which is the subject</p>\n<p>of the insurance.</p>\n<p>Macaura v Northern Assurance Co. Ltd. [1925] All ER</p>\n<p>Rep 51: This case establishes the principle that a shareholder</p>\n<p>has no insurable interest in the assets of a company. A</p>\n<p>shareholder in a company owning timber was owed a large sum</p>\n<p>of money by the company, and he insured the timber against</p>\n<p>loss.</p>\n<p>A fire occurred and he claimed from the insurers, but</p>\n<p>they repudiated liability on the ground that he had no</p>\n<p>insurable interest in the subject matter.</p>\n<p>Lord Sumner reasoned that the appellant had no insurable</p>\n<p>interest in the timber – it belonged to the company, Irish</p>\n<p>Canadian Sawmill Co. Limited of Skibbereen. The appellant</p>\n<p>had no security or lien over it, and although it lay on his</p>\n<p>land by his permission – he had no responsibility to its</p>\n<p>owner for its safety, nor was he under any contract that</p>\n<p>enabled him to hold it for his debt.</p>\n<p>He owned almost all the shares in the company, and the</p>\n<p>company owed him a great deal of money but neither as a</p>\n<p>creditor nor as a shareholder could he insure the</p>\n<p>company’s assets. The debt was not exposed to fire, neither</p>\n<p>were the shares – and the fact that he was the company’s</p>\n<p>only creditor, while the timber was its only asset, seemed to</p>\n<p>make no difference. He stood in no</p>\n<p>‘legal or equitable relation to’ the timber at all. He had no</p>\n<p>‘concern in’ the subject insured. His relation was to the</p>\n<p>company, not to its goods, and after the fire he was directly</p>\n<p>prejudiced by the paucity of the company’s assets, not by</p>\n<p>the fire.</p>\n<p>d. Control: it is essential that the event insured against is</p>\n<p>outside the control of the party assuming the risk. Read St.</p>\n<p>Christopher &amp; Medical Defence Union: “from Gould</p>\n<p>v. Curtis, especially the judgment of Buckley L.J. at</p>\n<p>p. 95, and from West Wake Price &amp; Co. v. Ching</p>\n<p>[1957] 1 W.L.R. 45, 51, it appears that there are two</p>\n<p>categories of insurance which may respectively be called</p>\n<p>indemnity insurance and contingency insurance. Indemnity</p>\n<p>insurance provides an indemnity against loss, as in a fire</p>\n<p>policy or a marine policy on a vessel. Within the limits of the</p>\n<p>policy the measure of the loss is the measure of the</p>\n<p>payment. Contingency insurance provides no indemnity but</p>\n<p>instead a payment upon a contingent event, as in a life policy</p>\n<p>or a personal injury policy. The sum to be paid is not</p>\n<p>measured by the loss but is stated in the policy. The</p>\n<p>contractual sum is paid if the life ends or the limb is lost,</p>\n<p>irrespective of the value of the life or the limb. With these</p>\n<p>two categories of insurance in mind, the three elements in a</p>\n<p>contract of insurance may be expressed as follows: and in</p>\n<p>this I draw largely on what Channell J. said in the Prudential</p>\n<p>case [1904] 2 K.B. 658, 663. First, the contract must</p>\n<p>provide that the assured will become entitled to</p>\n<p>something on the occurrence of some event. This, of</p>\n<p>course, is the disputed element, and the dispute is about</p>\n<p>what the “something” is…Second, the event must be one</p>\n<p>which involves some element of uncertainty… Third,</p>\n<p>the assured must have an insurable interest in the</p>\n<p>subject matter of the contract.”</p>\n<p>- Hellner, “The Scope of Insurance Regulation: What is</p>\n<p>Insurance for Purposes of Regulation?” (1963) 12 Am.</p>\n<p>J. Comp. L. 494 at 505.</p>\n<p>e. Provision of money’s worth: the provision of something</p>\n<p>other than money is enough, provided that it is of money’s</p>\n<p>worth – Department of Trade &amp; Industry</p>\n<h4 id=\"v-st-christopher-s-motorists-association-limited\">v St. Christopher’s Motorists’ Association Limited:</h4>\n<p>the defendant undertook to provide its members with</p>\n<p>chauffeur services should they be disqualified from driving</p>\n<p>due to being convicted of DUI. Court held that this</p>\n<p>constituted insurance and the fact that the benefits were not</p>\n<p>in money was irrelevant.</p>\n<p>Medical Defence Union v Department of Trade</p>\n<p>emphasizes that provision of services is not enough – there</p>\n<p>must be something that is clearly worth money, whether</p>\n<p>such is a right to valuable services, a right to advice – or a</p>\n<p>right to have an item of property repaired or replaced.</p>\n<h4 id=\"f-other-requirements\">f. Other Requirements:</h4>\n<p>Decisions including Hampton v Toxteth Cooperative</p>\n<p>Society [1915] 1 Ch 721; Hall D’Ath v British</p>\n<p>Provident Association (1932) 48 TLR 240 “appear to</p>\n<p>suggest” that there can be no insurance business carried out</p>\n<p>in the absence of a clearly stipulated premium and policy.</p>\n<h3 id=\"2-discuss-the-relationship-between-insurance-law-and\">2. Discuss the relationship between insurance law and</h3>\n<p>contract law.</p>\n<p>Formation of the contract</p>\n<p>Formation and formalities of an insurance contract are not</p>\n<p>governed by explicit rules of law. In practice, however, there are</p>\n<p>two standard ways for the initial agreement to enter into an</p>\n<p>insurance contract.</p>\n<p>Offer and acceptance</p>\n<p>First, this can be done through completion of a proposal form –</p>\n<p>which is a standard form produced by the insurers.</p>\n<p>The other way involves the proposer (or an intermediary such as a</p>\n<p>broker) using some other document. This is usually referred to as</p>\n<p>an insurance slip.</p>\n<p>Once the contract is accepted, there is a binding contract – even</p>\n<p>though a formal policy may not have yet been issued, provided</p>\n<p>that all material terms are accepted – Adie &amp; Sons v Insurances Corpn</p>\n<p>(1898) 14 TLR 544. These material terms are the amount and</p>\n<p>subject matter of the insurance and the commencement and</p>\n<p>duration of the risk. In most cases these</p>\n<p>matters will be evident from the proposal form and the</p>\n<p>surrounding circumstances. In addition, the premium must be</p>\n<p>quantified. See Alliss-Chalmers Co v Fidelity and Deposit Co of</p>\n<p>Maryland (1916) 114 LT 433.</p>\n<p>The insured is regarded as applying for the ordinary form of</p>\n<p>policy issued by the insurers – General Accident Insurance Corporation</p>\n<p>v Cronk (1901) 17 TLR 233.</p>\n<p>If the insurers do not unequivocally accept the insured’s</p>\n<p>proposal, but their acceptance is qualified by subjection to the</p>\n<p>payment of a payment or the issue of a policy, this constitutes a</p>\n<p>fresh offer by the insurer – which the insured must accept</p>\n<p>before a binding contract comes into existence: It may even be</p>\n<p>only an invitation to treat, so that the proposer must make a new</p>\n<p>offer on this basis: Locker v Law Union and Rock Insurance Co Ltd</p>\n<h2 id=\"1928-1-kb-554\">[1928] 1 KB 554.</h2>\n<p>Form of the contract</p>\n<p>Even as there is no general requirement for an insurance contract</p>\n<p>to be in writing, it is important to note that the general principles</p>\n<p>of contract law still apply to contracts of insurance. Oral contracts</p>\n<p>of insurance can be effective at common law, provided that an</p>\n<p>agreement on the material terms can be proved - Murfitt v Royal</p>\n<p>Insurance Co. (1922) 38 TLR 334.</p>\n<p>In practice, contracts must be in writing – as the Contracts Act</p>\n<p>requires that contracts of guarantee or indemnity have to be in</p>\n<p>writing – section 10(6) of the contracts Act. “Indemnity” is</p>\n<p>defined as an undertaking whereby a person agrees to reimburse</p>\n<p>another on the occurrence of an anticipated loss.</p>\n<p>Consideration</p>\n<p>The consideration given by the insured in return for the insurer’s</p>\n<p>undertaking to cover the risks insured against is called the</p>\n<p>premium - Lewis v Norwich Union Fire Insurance Co [1916] AC 509.</p>\n<h3 id=\"3-discuss-the-difference-between-an-insurance-contract-and\">3. Discuss the difference between an insurance contract and</h3>\n<p>other types of indemnity contracts.</p>\n<h4 id=\"topic-2\">Topic 2:</h4>\n<h3 id=\"1-discuss-the-key-elements-of-an-insurance-contract\">1. Discuss the key elements of an insurance contract.</h3>\n<p>An insurance contract is one under which one party (the insurer) in</p>\n<p>exchange for a premium – agrees with another party (the policy holder)</p>\n<p>to make a payment or provide a benefit to the policy holder or another</p>\n<p>person on the occurrence of a specified uncertain event which – if it</p>\n<p>occurs – would be adverse to the interests of the person who will</p>\n<p>receive the payment or benefit – The Insurance Act, 2017: Section</p>\n<p>2.</p>\n<h4 id=\"elements-of-an-insurance-contract\">Elements of an insurance contract:</h4>\n<p>(a) a binding contract – the insurer has to be legally bound to</p>\n<p>compensate the assured.</p>\n<p>Medical Defence Union v Department of Trade [1979] 1 Lloyd’s Rep</p>\n<p>499</p>\n<p>(b)       uncertainty as to whether the event assured against will</p>\n<p>occur or when it will occur;</p>\n<p>Gould v Curtis [1913] 3 KB 84</p>\n<p>(c) insurable interest – the insured is required to have an</p>\n<p>insurable interest in the subject matter of the insurance;</p>\n<p>Macaura v Northern Assurance Co. Limited [1925] All ER 51</p>\n<p>(d)       control – the event assured against must be outside the</p>\n<p>control of the party assuming the risk;</p>\n<p>St. Christopher v Medical Defence Union (e)the</p>\n<p>provision of money, or money’s worth</p>\n<p>Department of Trade &amp; Industry v St. Christopher’s Motorists’</p>\n<p>Association Limited</p>\n<p>The factors that influenced the development of Insurance Law in</p>\n<p>England up to 1902 become relevant for the development of insurance</p>\n<p>in Uganda at the commencement.</p>\n<p>The concept of insurance arose out of the mercantile practice of</p>\n<p>transporting goods across the sea – with an enormous fortune to be</p>\n<p>made if the project was successful, as contrasted with the disastrous</p>\n<p>loss if the project foundered amid the perils of the seas. Accordingly,</p>\n<p>the common law of insurance developed in the first instance through</p>\n<p>decisions on marine insurance. Non-marine insurance made its first</p>\n<p>appearance in the form of life and fire insurance, and these three types</p>\n<p>of insurance formed the whole range of insurance until the 19th</p>\n<p>Century – 8 Holdsworth&#x27;s History of English Law (2nd Edn) 294 et seq.</p>\n<p>Why do we have the law as we have it? What are the interests this law</p>\n<p>promotes/protects?</p>\n<p>Demonstrate the key developments and explain why they occurred.</p>\n<p>Insurance developed as a mechanism of spreading risk across a</p>\n<p>larger pool of risk-bearers.</p>\n<p>-  Factors that informed the change in regulation</p>\n<p>-  What is the present form of regulation?</p>\n<h3 id=\"2-judges-often-cite-business-to-reach-decisions-in-insurance-and\">2. Judges often cite business to reach decisions in insurance, and</h3>\n<p>other commercial cases. Briefly explain the business sense</p>\n<p>underlying insurance law. You should, amongst other explore the</p>\n<p>following notions: insurance being a risk management tool; the</p>\n<p>premium account being a pool to which many persons (insureds)</p>\n<p>have contributed, as security for a rainy day; and the significance</p>\n<p>of probability of perils insured against. This should help us</p>\n<p>understand why many of the principles and rules of insurance law</p>\n<p>are the way they are.</p>\n<h3 id=\"3-discuss-the-key-factors-that-have-underpinned-the-historical\">3. Discuss the key factors that have underpinned the historical</h3>\n<p>development of insurance law in Uganda?</p>\n<h4 id=\"topic-3\">Topic 3:</h4>\n<h3 id=\"4-discuss-the-prerequisites-of-a-valid-insurance-contract\">4. Discuss the prerequisites of a valid insurance contract.</h3>\n<h3 id=\"5-discuss-the-principles-governing-formation-of-insurance\">5. Discuss the principles governing formation of insurance</h3>\n<p>contracts.</p>\n<p>Insurance is regulated by legislation, for which reason it has a</p>\n<p>statutory definition in section 2 of the Insurance Act, 2017. The</p>\n<p>general law of contract governs contracts of insurance, subject to</p>\n<p>some additional special principles applicable to insurance.</p>\n<p>These principles are common to both marine and non-marine</p>\n<p>insurance.</p>\n<h4 id=\"indemnity\">Indemnity:</h4>\n<p>Modern contracts of insurance belong to the general category of</p>\n<p>contracts of indemnity: this means that the insurer’s liability is</p>\n<p>limited to actual loss, which is in fact proved. Cases on this point</p>\n<p>include Darrell v Tibbits (1880) 5 QBD 560; Castellain v Preston</p>\n<p>(1883) 11 QBD 380; Meacock v Bryant &amp;</p>\n<p>Co. [1942] 2 All ER 661; Haghiran v Allied Dunbar Insurance [2001] 1</p>\n<p>All ER (Comm) 97, CA.</p>\n<p>This means that the occurrence of the event does not of itself</p>\n<p>entitle the insured to the payment of the sum stipulated in the</p>\n<p>policy – Dane v Mortgage Insurance Corpn Ltd [1894] 1 QB 54 at 61,</p>\n<p>CA, per Lord Esher MR; West Wake Price &amp; Co v Ching [1956] 3 All</p>\n<p>ER 821 at 825, [1957] 1 WLR 45 at 49 per</p>\n<p>Devlin J.</p>\n<p>The event must in fact result into a pecuniary loss to the insured</p>\n<p>– Garden v Ingram (1852) 23 LJCh 478 at 479 – who then becomes</p>\n<p>entitled to be indemnified subject to the limitations of his contract –</p>\n<p>Dalby v India and London Life Assurance Co (1854) 15 CB 365, Ex Ch.</p>\n<p>He cannot recover more than the sum insured, for that sum is all</p>\n<p>that he has stipulated for by his premiums and it fixes the</p>\n<p>maximum liability of the insurers – Westminster Fire Office v Glasgow</p>\n<p>Provident Investment Society (1888) 13 App Cas 699 at 711, HL, per</p>\n<p>Lord Selborne LC; cf Curtis &amp; Sons v Mathews (1918) as reported in</p>\n<h2 id=\"35-tlr-189-ca\">35 TLR 189, CA.</h2>\n<p>Even within that limit, he cannot recover more than what he</p>\n<p>establishes to be the actual amount of his loss – Westminster Fire</p>\n<p>Office v Glasgow Provident Investment Society (1888) 13 App Cas 699 at</p>\n<p>711, HL, per Lord Selborne LC; cf Curtis &amp; Sons v Mathews (1918) as</p>\n<p>reported in 35 TLR 189, CA.</p>\n<p>The contract being one of indemnity, and indemnity alone, he can</p>\n<p>recover the actual amount of his loss and no more – Castellain v</p>\n<p>Preston (1883) 11 QBD 380 at 386, CA, per Brett LJ; whatever may</p>\n<p>have been his estimate of what his loss would likely be, and</p>\n<p>whatever the premiums he may have paid, calculated on the basis</p>\n<p>of that estimate.</p>\n<p>In all contracts of insurance, whether marine or non-marine,</p>\n<p>which are contracts of indemnity, the insurer is entitled to be</p>\n<p>subrogated to the rights of the insured, and to a contribution from</p>\n<p>other insurers where he has paid the whole of the loss or more</p>\n<p>than his proportionate share of it.</p>\n<h4 id=\"the-doctrine-of-subrogation\">The doctrine of subrogation:</h4>\n<p>Subrogation expresses the right of the insurer to be placed in the</p>\n<p>position of the insured so as to be entitled to the</p>\n<p>advantage of all the rights and remedies which the insured</p>\n<p>possesses against third parties in respect of the subject matter -</p>\n<p>Castellain v Preston (1883) 11 QBD 380 at 388, CA, per Brett LJ.</p>\n<h4 id=\"the-principle-of-subrogation-may-be-illustrated-as-follows\">The principle of subrogation may be illustrated as follows:</h4>\n<p>If two ships, A and B, come into collision and the</p>\n<p>collision is due to the negligence of those in charge of B,</p>\n<p>the owner of A, who has insured her, can recover the</p>\n<p>amount of his loss from the owner of B, and the owner of B</p>\n<p>cannot resist the claim on the ground that the owner of A</p>\n<p>was entitled to recover or had already recovered from his</p>\n<p>insurers. If the owner of A has recovered his loss from</p>\n<p>the owner of B, he will be compelled to account to the</p>\n<p>insurers for the money he has so received, and his claim</p>\n<p>against them will be to that extent reduced or, if he has</p>\n<p>recovered a complete indemnity, extinguished. Although</p>\n<p>he is bound to account to the insurers for the money</p>\n<p>received, he does not hold it as trustee for them: Stearns v</p>\n<p>Village Main Reef Gold Mining Co (1905) 10 Com Cas 89,</p>\n<p>CA, applying the principle of Randal v Cockran (1748) 1 Ves</p>\n<p>Sen 98. The owner of A may, however, in the first</p>\n<p>instance recover his loss from the insurers, in which case</p>\n<p>they will be subrogated to his right to recover the loss</p>\n<p>from the owner of B.</p>\n<p>The precise nature of the third party’s liability to the insured is</p>\n<p>immaterial: subrogation applies even to a statutory liability –</p>\n<p>Ellerbeck Collieries Ltd v Cornhill Insurance Co Ltd [1932] 1 KB 401 at</p>\n<p>411, CA, per Scrutton LJ.</p>\n<p>If the third parties are insured, the ultimate liability for the loss</p>\n<p>falls on their insurers – North British and Mercantile Insurance Co v</p>\n<p>London, Liverpool and Globe Insurance Co (1877) 5 ChD 569, CA.</p>\n<p>The right does not arise until the insurers have admitted their liability</p>\n<p>to the insured - Midland Insurance Co v Smith (1881)</p>\n<p>6 QBD 561 at 564 per Watkin Williams J; Page v Scottish</p>\n<p>Insurance Corpn Ltd (1929) 140 LT 571, CA., and have paid him all</p>\n<p>amounts due in respect of the loss – Castellain v Preston.</p>\n<h4 id=\"subrogation-in-marine-insurance\">Subrogation in marine insurance:</h4>\n<p>Where the insurer pays for a total loss, either of the whole or (in</p>\n<p>the case of goods) any apportionable part of the subject matter</p>\n<p>insured, he becomes entitled to take over the interest of the</p>\n<p>assured in whatever may remain the subject matter so paid for –</p>\n<p>and he is therefore subrogated to all the rights and remedies of the</p>\n<p>assured in respect of that subject matter as from the time of the</p>\n<p>casualty causing the loss.</p>\n<p>▪ Section 63(1) of the Marine Insurance Act, 1906; the</p>\n<p>insurer is not bound to take the interest over, but they</p>\n<p>have a choice whether to do so or not.</p>\n<p>Subject to these provisions, where the insurer pays for a partial</p>\n<p>loss, he acquires no title to the subject matter insured, or such part</p>\n<p>of it as may remain – but he is thereupon subrogated to all the</p>\n<p>rights and remedies of the assured in and in respect of the</p>\n<p>subject matter insured as from the time of the peril causing the</p>\n<p>loss, insofar as the assured has been indemnified by that payment</p>\n<p>for the loss.</p>\n<p>Therefore, when a loss happens, anything which reduces or</p>\n<p>diminishes it reduces or diminishes the amount the insurer is bound</p>\n<p>to pay; and if the insurer has already paid the full loss, then if</p>\n<p>anything which diminishes the loss afterwards comes into the</p>\n<p>hands of the assured, the insurer is entitled to be recouped to the</p>\n<p>extent of the benefit so received – Burnand v Rodocanachi Sons &amp; Co</p>\n<p>(1882) 7 App Cas 333 at 339, HL, per Lord Blackburn; see also Darrell v</p>\n<p>Tibbitts (1880) 5 QBD 560, CA, and the judgments in Castellain v Preston</p>\n<p>(1883) 11 QBD 380, CA (where the principle of subrogation is explained); H</p>\n<p>Cousins &amp; Co Ltd v D and C Carriers Ltd [1971] 2 QB 230, [1971]</p>\n<p>1 All ER 55, CA.</p>\n<p>This doctrine applies to all contracts of non-marine insurance, which</p>\n<p>are contracts of indemnity e.g., fire insurance (Castellain v Preston</p>\n<p>[1883] 11 QBD 380), motor insurance (Horse, Carriage and General</p>\n<p>Insurance Co. Limited v Petch</p>\n<p>[1916] 33 TLR 131) and contingency insurance covering non-</p>\n<p>payment of money (Meacock v Bryant &amp; Co. [1942] 2 All ER 661).</p>\n<p>It applies, whether the loss is total, partial and is a corollary of</p>\n<p>the principle of indemnity. By requiring any means of diminishing</p>\n<p>or extinguishing a loss to be taken into account, it prevents the</p>\n<p>insured from recovering more than a full indemnity – Castellain v</p>\n<p>Preston (1883) 11 QBD 380 at 386, CA, per Brett LJ.</p>\n<p>It does not apply to contracts of life insurance and personal accident</p>\n<p>insurance. See Dalby v India &amp; London Life Assurance Co (1854) 15</p>\n<p>CB 365; Law v London Indisputable Life Policy Co (1855) 1 K &amp; J 223.</p>\n<p>On personal accident insurance – see Bramwell, B in Bradburn v Great</p>\n<p>Western Rly Co (1874) LR 10 Exch 1 at 2.</p>\n<h3 id=\"6-explain-the-practical-steps-and-documents-involved-in-the\">6. Explain the practical steps and documents involved in the</h3>\n<p>process of formation of insurance contracts.</p>\n<h3 id=\"7-construction-of-insurance-contracts-discuss-the-rules\">7. Construction of Insurance Contracts: Discuss the rules</h3>\n<p>and principles of construction of insurance contracts.</p>\n<p>1st November 2022 – Questions 4, 5 &amp; 6</p>\n<h3 id=\"1-discuss-the-prerequisites-of-a-valid-insurance-contract\">1. Discuss the prerequisites of a valid insurance contract.</h3>\n<h3 id=\"2-discuss-the-principles-governing-formation-of-insurance-contracts\">2. Discuss the principles governing formation of insurance contracts.</h3>\n<p>The question is concerned with principles governing formation of</p>\n<p>a contract – not principles of insurance in general. The insurance</p>\n<p>equivalents to principles regulating contracts in general.</p>\n<p>▪ Offer: in a normal insurance contracting process, where the</p>\n<p>insurer issues a proposal form.</p>\n<h4 id=\"acceptance\">▪ Acceptance:</h4>\n<h4 id=\"consideration\">▪ Consideration:</h4>\n<p>▪ The contract is concluded when the policy is</p>\n<p>issued. (Upon full payment of premium)</p>\n<p>▪ The intention to create legal relations</p>\n<p>▪ Certainty of terms</p>\n<p>▪ Legality of purpose</p>\n<p>▪ Utmost good faith</p>\n<p>▪ Insurable interest</p>\n<h3 id=\"3-explain-the-practical-steps-and-documents-involved-in-the\">3. Explain the practical steps and documents involved in the</h3>\n<p>process of formation of insurance contracts.</p>\n<h4 id=\"castellain-v-preston-1883-11-qbd-380\">Castellain v Preston (1883) 11 QBD 380:</h4>\n<p>Plaintiff sued on behalf of an insurance company to recover money with</p>\n<p>interest. The defendants insured their buildings against loss by fire, and</p>\n<p>paid the requisite premiums until after the fire occurred.</p>\n<p>In July 1878, the defendants sold the land and buildings to their</p>\n<p>tenants – and the contract provided that at the time of the completion</p>\n<p>(of the sale) should be within two years, chosen by the vendors. In</p>\n<p>August 1878, the buildings were partly damaged by fire, and the</p>\n<p>defendants made a claim. The amount was in fact paid by the insurers</p>\n<p>– who did not know about the existence of a contract of sale.</p>\n<p>In March 1879, the contract was completed – and in December, the</p>\n<p>conveyance was executed and the balance of the purchase money was</p>\n<p>paid.</p>\n<p>The plaintiff contended that a policy of fire insurance is a contract of</p>\n<p>indemnity, and the assured cannot derive a profit because the thing</p>\n<p>insured has been damaged by the peril insured against – Darrell v</p>\n<p>Tibbitts 5 QBD 560. Further, they contended that the insurer is entitled</p>\n<p>to every benefit whereby the loss is diminished.</p>\n<p>The defendants were to be regarded in the same position as a ship-</p>\n<p>owner, who refuses to give up the salvage of a sunk ship to its</p>\n<p>underwriters, even when he has received the amount due – yet the</p>\n<p>assured cannot withhold the salvage from the insurer – Arnould on</p>\n<p>Marine Insurance, part 3, ch. 6, pp. 935, 936.</p>\n<p>The defendants argued: indeed, a contract of insurance is, prima facie, a</p>\n<p>contract of indemnity, and the insurer is entitled to be subrogated into</p>\n<p>the rights of the assured which arise out of contracts entered into with</p>\n<p>respect of preservation of the subject- matter of insurance – but Darrell</p>\n<p>v Tibbits 5 QBD 560 did not apply to the instant case.</p>\n<p>The contract for sale of the house was not one with respect to its</p>\n<p>maintenance in good condition and its preservation from</p>\n<p>destruction by fire. The defendants were unpaid vendors at the time</p>\n<p>when the loss happened, and the plaintiff’s company could have had</p>\n<p>no defence to an action/claim against the policy – Collingridge v Royal</p>\n<p>Exchange Assurance Corporation 3 QBD 560. It was of no</p>\n<p>consequence that at the completion of the contract, the defendants’</p>\n<p>rights in the property would cease.</p>\n<h4 id=\"bowen-lj\">Bowen, LJ:</h4>\n<p>Simpson v Thompson, 3 App. Cas. 279 establishes that “an insurer is</p>\n<p>only subrogated to those rights which may be enforced against third</p>\n<p>persons in respect of the loss: in the present case the defendants, as</p>\n<p>vendors, had no rights as against the purchasers in respect of the loss;</p>\n<p>for a contract of sale is quite distinct from a contract of insurance –</p>\n<p>Edwards v. West 7 Ch. D. 858.</p>\n<p>“A fire policy is not quite a contract of indemnity, and a distinction exists</p>\n<p>between fire insurances and marine insurances; but it has been held that</p>\n<p>even a marine policy is not a complete contract of indemnity: Aitchison</p>\n<p>v. Lohre 4 App. Cas. 755.</p>\n<p>A gift is not to be take into account between the assured and the</p>\n<p>insurer as diminishing the loss – Burnand v Rodocanachi, 7 App.</p>\n<p>Cas. 333.</p>\n<p>“The doctrine of subrogation only applies where the insurer can bring</p>\n<p>an action in the name of the assured: here the defendants have received</p>\n<p>no money for the use of the plaintiff company, and therefore no action</p>\n<p>for money had and received will lie against them.”</p>\n<h4 id=\"brett-lj\">Brett, LJ:</h4>\n<p>He noted that the purchasers, under the contract of sale, would have</p>\n<p>to buy the house upon notice, whether it had burned down or not. The</p>\n<p>house burned down before the date of payment.</p>\n<p>Brett, LJ noted that the defendants had an insurable interest, because at</p>\n<p>all times they were the legal owners of the property, and it was possible</p>\n<p>that the buyers might not fulfil the contract – and the</p>\n<p>vendors/defendants would suffer loss. The plaintiffs paid the defendants</p>\n<p>money for the loss, and the contract of sale was subsequently concluded</p>\n<p>for the full amount.</p>\n<p>The claim of the plaintiff was not to recover money paid by the</p>\n<p>insurance company, but he nonetheless brought the action in respect</p>\n<p>of the money. The trial court had dismissed the case on the ground</p>\n<p>that “the doctrine of subrogation of the insurer into the position of the</p>\n<p>assured is confined within limits, which prevent it from extending to</p>\n<p>the present case.”</p>\n<p>He then proceeded to assess ‘every rule which has been promulgated</p>\n<h4 id=\"and-acted-on-by-the-courts-with-regard-to-insurance-law\">and acted on by the courts with regard to insurance law’:</h4>\n<h3 id=\"1-the-principle-of-indemnity\">1.    The Principle of Indemnity:</h3>\n<p>“The very foundation, in my opinion, of every rule which has been</p>\n<p>applied to insurance law is this, namely, that the contract of insurance</p>\n<p>contained in a marine or fire policy is a contract of indemnity, and of</p>\n<p>indemnity only, and that this contract means that the assured, in case of</p>\n<p>a loss against which the policy has been made, shall be fully</p>\n<p>indemnified, but shall never be more than fully indemnified. That is the</p>\n<p>fundamental principle of insurance, and if ever a proposition is</p>\n<p>brought forward which is at variance with it, that is to say, which either</p>\n<p>will prevent the assured from obtaining a full indemnity, or which will</p>\n<p>give to the assured more than a full indemnity, that proposition must</p>\n<p>certainly be wrong.”</p>\n<h3 id=\"2-constructive-total-loss-under-marine-insurance\">2.    Constructive total loss under Marine Insurance</h3>\n<p>This originated to carry out the principle of indemnity, introduced for</p>\n<p>the benefit of the assured, and a constructive total loss is equivalent to</p>\n<p>an actual loss, lest the assured would not recover a full indemnity.</p>\n<h3 id=\"3-the-doctrine-of-abandonment\">3.    The doctrine of Abandonment</h3>\n<p>This is grafted upon the doctrine of constructive total loss, and operates</p>\n<p>in favour of the underwriter, in order that the assured may not recover</p>\n<p>more than full indemnity. It is difficult to justify in principle, and was</p>\n<p>introduced as a matter of justice to prevent the assured to obtain more</p>\n<p>than full indemnity by fraud.</p>\n<p>“That doctrine is to a certain extent technical, that is to say, although</p>\n<p>the assured has in reality suffered a constructive total loss, and</p>\n<p>although he is upon general principles entitled to recover,</p>\n<p>nevertheless he must fail unless he has given a notice of abandonment.”</p>\n<h3 id=\"4-the-doctrine-of-subrogation\">4.   The doctrine of Subrogation</h3>\n<p>This does not arise upon any of the terms of the contract of insurance,</p>\n<p>it is only another proposition which has been adopted to for purposes</p>\n<p>of indemnity. It is a doctrine in favour of the underwriters or insurers</p>\n<p>in order to prevent the assured from recovering more than a full</p>\n<p>indemnity; it has been adopted solely for that reason. It is not applied</p>\n<p>to insurance law on the ground that underwriters are sureties, as they</p>\n<p>aren’t always so.</p>\n<p>The question now, was whether this doctrine can be limited in any way</p>\n<p>i.e., that the underwriter is subrogated into the place of the assured so</p>\n<p>far as to enable the underwriter to enforce a contract, or to enforce a</p>\n<p>right of action? Why is it to be limited to that, if when it is limited to</p>\n<p>that, it will, in certain cases, enable the assured to recover more than a</p>\n<p>full indemnity?</p>\n<p>It would be ‘contrary to the foundation of the law of insurance’ if</p>\n<p>subrogation has the effect of enabling an assured to recover more than a</p>\n<p>full indemnity.</p>\n<p>The trial judge limited the doctrine of subrogation to placing the</p>\n<p>insurer in the position of the assured only for the purpose of enforcing</p>\n<p>a right of action to which the assured is entitled. “…the full and</p>\n<p>absolute meaning of the word must be used, that is to say, the insurer</p>\n<p>must be placed in the position of the assured…as between the</p>\n<p>underwriter and the assured, the underwriter is entitled to the</p>\n<p>advantage of every right of the assured, whether such right consists in</p>\n<p>contract, fulfilled or unfulfilled, or in remedy for tort capable…or in</p>\n<p>any other right…legal or equitable…whether such right could or could</p>\n<p>not be enforced by the insurer in the name of the assured by the</p>\n<p>exercise or acquiring of which right or condition the loss against which</p>\n<p>the assured is insured, can be, or has been diminished.”</p>\n<p>“The doctrine of subrogation goes much further than a mere transfer</p>\n<p>of those rights, which may at any time give a cause of action either in</p>\n<p>contract or tort, because if upon the happening of the loss there is a</p>\n<p>contract between the assured and a third person, and if that contract is</p>\n<p>immediately fulfilled by the third person,</p>\n<p>then there in no right of action of any kind into which the insurer can</p>\n<p>be subrogated. The right of action is gone; the contract is fulfilled. In</p>\n<p>like manner if upon the happening of a tort the tort is immediately</p>\n<p>made good by the tort feasor, then the right of action is gone; there is</p>\n<p>no right of action existing into which the insurer can be subrogated. It</p>\n<p>will be said that there did for a moment exist a right of action in favour</p>\n<p>of the assured, into which the insurer could have been subrogated. But</p>\n<p>he cannot be subrogated into a right of action until he has paid the sum</p>\n<p>insured and made good the loss. Therefore, innumerable cases would be</p>\n<p>taken out of the doctrine, if it were to be confined to existing rights of</p>\n<p>action.”</p>\n<p>Having assessed a number of passages from other precedents, Brett</p>\n<p>LJ concluded that ‘it was necessary that the plaintiff should succeed in</p>\n<p>this case.’</p>\n<p>“The doctrine of subrogation must be carried out to the full extent, and</p>\n<p>carried out in this case by enabling the plaintiff to recover.”</p>\n<p>In National Insurance Corporation Limited v Kakugu, the respondent insured</p>\n<p>three motorcycles which were later stolen. The appellant contended</p>\n<p>that they were not entitled to indemnity for non-disclosure of</p>\n<p>particulars including; a hire-purchase agreement, the true identity of the</p>\n<p>rider of the motorcycle when it was stolen and they alleged connivance</p>\n<p>between the thief and the respondent’s employee.</p>\n<p>Court noted that good faith duties are significantly more onerous for</p>\n<p>the insured and it is unfair because (a) the insured can be unaware of</p>\n<p>their duty to volunteer information not specifically requested on the</p>\n<p>proposal form; (b) the law requires the insured to ensure whether</p>\n<p>information would be relevant to a prudent underwriter’s assessment</p>\n<p>of risk; (c) breach can arise out of a ‘reasonable’ error on the part of</p>\n<p>the insured, such as lack of technical knowledge (d) the only remedy</p>\n<p>for breach of good faith duties is retrospective avoidance of the entire</p>\n<p>contract and (e) the insurer is not required to show that non-disclosure</p>\n<p>or misrepresentation had any causal link to the claim so as to avoid the</p>\n<p>contract and (e) intermediaries such as brokers are not held responsible</p>\n<p>for any failings on their part.</p>\n<p>The nondisclosure and misrepresentation alleged would not have</p>\n<p>changed the decision of the insurer to give out the policy.</p>\n<p>Tuesday, 6th December 2022 – Mr. Robert Kirunda</p>\n<p>Mac Gillivary and Parkington, (1975) Insurance Law, 6th</p>\n<p>Edition, Sweet and Maxwell</p>\n<p>Collinvaux’s Law of Insurance, Sweet and Maxwell, 7th</p>\n<p>Edition</p>\n<p>J. Lowry and Phillip Rawlings (2004), Insurance Law, Cases</p>\n<p>and Materials</p>\n<h4 id=\"topics-to-be-covered\">Topics to be covered:</h4>\n<p>▪   Insurable Interest</p>\n<p>▪   Premium</p>\n<p>▪   Risk</p>\n<p>▪   Conditions, Warranties and Causation</p>\n<p>▪   Claims</p>\n<p>▪   Good faith</p>\n<h2 id=\"good-faith\">GOOD FAITH</h2>\n<p>The assured is in the unique position of having an understanding of the</p>\n<p>factors necessitating their insurance, and the insurer is in position to be</p>\n<p>able to assess the risk. Both parties need accurate information in this</p>\n<p>regard.</p>\n<p>A proposal form solicits information in order for the insurer to</p>\n<p>understand the risk: there has to be some uncertainty that there is going</p>\n<p>to be an event against which the (prospective) assured seeks indemnity.</p>\n<p>The insurer examines a spectrum of factors to decide whether to take</p>\n<p>on the risk.</p>\n<p>The insurer expects that every question asked will be answered, and</p>\n<p>answered truthfully.</p>\n<p>The corollary obligation to the disclosure is that a skilled insurer will</p>\n<p>ask further questions if needed.</p>\n<p>The facts that need to be disclosed are material. The decision: “Would a</p>\n<p>reasonable insurer rely on this fact to take the policy?”</p>\n<p>If the fact is not solicited, the assumption on the part of the assured is</p>\n<p>that it was not required.</p>\n<p>▪ Does the assumption always hold?</p>\n<p>▪ Does it matter that non-disclosure was a mistake?</p>\n<p>Joel v Law Union (1908) 2 K.B 415</p>\n<p>-   CTI Inc v. Oceanus Mutual Underwriting Association (1984) 1 Lloyd’s</p>\n<p>Rep. 467</p>\n<p>-   Pan Atlantic Insurance Co Ltd v Pine Top (1994) 3 All ER 581 This</p>\n<p>case discusses the materiality of a misrepresentation. The House</p>\n<p>of Lords also clarified the law relating to the right of avoidance</p>\n<p>and has, in upholding the ‘actual inducement’ test, rejected the</p>\n<p>‘decisive influence’ test.</p>\n<p>-   Christine Mawadri v Britt Syndicates &amp; Anor HCCS 376/2009 Plaintiff</p>\n<p>filed the suit for recovery of about $500,000 as loss incurred</p>\n<p>following cancellation and postponement of a music event she</p>\n<p>promoted and insured. She had entered into a music promotion</p>\n<p>agreement with M/s Scangroup Limited – who had acquired the</p>\n<p>rights to stage musical concerts featuring Akon from the</p>\n<p>American Talent Group.</p>\n<p>The plaintiff paid $350,000 for Akon to perform in Uganda –</p>\n<p>and was responsible for selling tickets and supervising gate</p>\n<p>collections on the actual concert day.</p>\n<p>She undertook to get insurance with the second defendant for a</p>\n<p>“no show” or non-appearance of the artist due to cancellation,</p>\n<p>postponement or any other reason: and the cover note was signed</p>\n<p>on 14th April 2008.</p>\n<p>The event was postponed because Akon had a medical condition.</p>\n<p>The plaintiff argued that this postponement caused her loss on</p>\n<p>ticket sales – and this was an insurable peril under the policy. The</p>\n<p>defendants contended that the policy did not cover loss arising</p>\n<p>from non-appearance of the artist and in any case, the plaintiff</p>\n<p>breached the insurance contract by not notifying the first</p>\n<p>defendant of the insurable loss within 72 hours under the policy –</p>\n<p>thus they were not entitled to any indemnity.</p>\n<p>Court found that whether or not the claim was payable or not is a factor of</p>\n<p>whether the events leading up to the loss was insured.</p>\n<p>It examined the role of the broker in the procurement of the</p>\n<p>insurance policy: The authors Raoul &amp; Colinvaux in their book</p>\n<p>The Law of Insurance 4th Ed (P 297 to 300) discuss the role and</p>\n<p>liability of insurance brokers. The learned authors make it clear</p>\n<p>that insurance brokers do owe their clients as prospective insured</p>\n<p>person a duty of care – they are under a duty to act carefully and</p>\n<p>also to exercise proper care and skill when carrying out the</p>\n<p>assured’s instructions or to be liable in damages, and possibly for</p>\n<p>the assured’s loss as would be an underweiter.</p>\n<h4 id=\"they-write-at-page-297\">They write (at Page 297):</h4>\n<h4 id=\"duty-of-assured-s-agent\">Duty of Assured’s Agent:</h4>\n<p>Such an agent, even though he acts gratuitously, is under a</p>\n<p>duty to the assured to act carefully, and is liable to take out</p>\n<p>insurance in favour of the assured and may be held liable in</p>\n<p>damages not only for what the insurer might have been</p>\n<p>legally liable to pay, in the event of a loss, but for what, on</p>\n<p>the balance of probabilities, the insurer would have paid.</p>\n<p>Insurance brokers are agents who make it their business to</p>\n<p>procure contracts of insurance for those who employ them.</p>\n<p>Having undertaken to obtain insurance an insurance broker</p>\n<p>must exercise proper care and skill in carrying out the</p>\n<p>assured’s instructions, and he cannot excuse himself from</p>\n<p>accepting a policy that gives insufficient cover by saying</p>\n<p>that the assured ought to have examined it. But there is no</p>\n<p>duty in law on him to notify of the terms of a cover note as</p>\n<p>soon as possible, though in practice it is usual for him to do</p>\n<p>so. As the assured’s agent, he should make inquiries as to</p>\n<p>material facts and will be liable to the assured for breach of</p>\n<p>duty if he (the broker) fails, through his lack of care in this</p>\n<p>matter, to disclose such facts as are material (e.g. claims</p>\n<p>history) with the result that the policy is avoided by the</p>\n<p>insurers…”</p>\n<p>Nature of action by assured against broker</p>\n<p>Where a broker is sued by his client for the failure by the</p>\n<p>broker to procure an effective contract of insurance for the</p>\n<p>client, the gist of the action against the broker is not the loss</p>\n<p>which the client may have suffered thereby but the broker’s</p>\n<p>breach of duty to him…”</p>\n<p>Where a person employs an insurance broker as opposed to</p>\n<p>going directly to an insurer, then the ordinary law of agency</p>\n<p>applies to that relationship - Halsbury’s Laws of England 4th ed</p>\n<p>(Para 381): “…it is usual for the insurance agent, not the proposer to fill in</p>\n<p>the proposal although the proposer’s signature is always necessary, and the</p>\n<p>completed and signed proposal is usually transmitted to the insurers by the</p>\n<p>agent. The position of such an agent has therefore given raise to specific</p>\n<p>insurance problems…”</p>\n<p>Part of the proposal form in this case was left blank – even when</p>\n<h4 id=\"signed-by-the-plaintiff-in-halsbury-s-laws-of-england-at-para-373-4\">signed by the plaintiff. In Halsbury’s Laws of England at para 373[4]:</h4>\n<p>Where the space for an answer is left blank, leaving the</p>\n<p>question unanswered, the reasonable inference may be that</p>\n<p>there is nothing to enter as an answer. If in fact there is</p>\n<p>something to enter as an answer, the insurers are misled in</p>\n<p>that their reasonable inference is belied. It will then be a</p>\n<p>matter of construction whether this is a mere non-</p>\n<p>disclosure, the proposer having made no positive statement</p>\n<p>at all or whether in substance he is to be regarded as having</p>\n<p>asserted that there is nothing to state…”</p>\n<p>This applied to the insurance broker. The position in relation to the first</p>\n<p>defendant was slightly different: On Lloyd’s Underwriters the authors</p>\n<p>Raoul and Colinvaux (supra at page 298 para 15-18) write</p>\n<p>“…Lloyd’s brokers hold a special position, since contracts of</p>\n<p>insurance with Lloyd’s underwriters cannot be made except with</p>\n<p>brokers who are members of Lloyd’s. Such brokers are, as is the</p>\n<p>general position, agents of the assured and not of the</p>\n<p>underwriters, but by a special custom of Lloyd’s the broker</p>\n<p>himself is liable to the underwriters for the premium, and has a</p>\n<p>lien on the policy in respect of it. He may in such cases be</p>\n<p>regarded as an agent of the insurers for the purpose only of</p>\n<p>receiving the premium…”</p>\n<p>In this case, evidence on record indicated that the plaintiff desired a</p>\n<p>policy which covered non-appearance and both their lawyer and</p>\n<p>broker knew this. There was no breach by D2 of their duty of case in</p>\n<p>procuring the policy she wanted: it would be “unreasonable to expect</p>\n<p>the second defendant to fill Part B of the proposal form for the</p>\n<p>plaintiff; that would be exceeding it duty as a broker in the</p>\n<p>circumstances. What can be said is that it was sloppy underwriting to</p>\n<p>send a form with a Part B signed but not filled in because this could</p>\n<p>and indeed lead to confusion.”</p>\n<p>The reasonable inference was that the plaintiff – by not filling in part B</p>\n<p>of the policy – did not have the necessary information: and if she did,</p>\n<p>but did not fill it in – this would amount to non-disclosure. The first</p>\n<p>defendant underwriters were accordingly correct not to provide a policy</p>\n<p>cover for non-appearance on the basis of the proposal form.</p>\n<p>The plaintiff was accordingly not entitled to $500,000 as claimed – and</p>\n<p>court did not have to ascertain the loss.</p>\n<h4 id=\"focus-on\">Focus on:</h4>\n<p>- Materiality – the Pine Top decision</p>\n<p>- Inducement</p>\n<p>- Mistake/Misrepresentation*</p>\n<p>Is there a correlation between mistake in the context of good faith and</p>\n<p>fraudulent disclosure in the context of claims? - Longway Suitcase</p>\n<p>Manufacturing Co. v UAP Insurance (U) Ltd [2014] UGCOMMC</p>\n<p>14;</p>\n<p>It is a fundamental principle of insurance law that the utmost</p>\n<p>good faith must be observed by each party. This rule was stated</p>\n<p>by Lord Mansfield in CARTER v BOEHM (1766) as</p>\n<p>follows: &quot;Insurance is a contract upon speculation. The special</p>\n<p>facts, upon which the contingent chance is to be computed, lie</p>\n<p>more commonly in the knowledge of the insured only: the</p>\n<p>underwriter trusts to his representation, and proceeds upon</p>\n<p>confidence that he does not keep back any circumstance in his</p>\n<p>knowledge, to mislead the underwriter into a belief that the</p>\n<p>circumstance does not exist, and to induce him to estimate the</p>\n<p>risk as if it did not exist. The keeping back such circumstance is a</p>\n<p>fraud, and therefore the policy is void. Although the suppression</p>\n<p>should happen through mistake without any fraudulent intent, yet</p>\n<p>still the underwriter is deceived, and the policy is void; because the</p>\n<p>risk run is really different from the risk understood and intended</p>\n<p>to be run at the time of the agreement..... the governing principle</p>\n<p>is applicable to all contracts and dealings. Good faith forbids</p>\n<p>either party by concealing what he privately knows, to draw the</p>\n<p>other into a bargain, from his ignorance of that fact, and his</p>\n<p>believing the contrary        &quot;</p>\n<p>According to Hodgins, misrepresentation is used to describe</p>\n<p>situations where the wrong or misleading answer has been given</p>\n<p>to questions posed of the applicant for insurance.</p>\n<p>It is difficult to discuss misrepresentation without discussing</p>\n<p>non-disclosure/Concealment. Non-disclosure describes a</p>\n<p>situation where no answer has been volunteered to the insurer</p>\n<p>because no specific question was asked.</p>\n<p>Generally speaking, in order for an insurance company to void a</p>\n<p>contract because of misrepresented information, the information</p>\n<p>in question must be material to the decision to extend coverage.</p>\n<p>Concealment, on the other hand, is the failure to disclose</p>\n<p>information that one clearly knows about. To void a contract on</p>\n<p>the grounds of concealment, the insurer typically must prove that</p>\n<p>the applicant willfully and intentionally concealed information</p>\n<p>that was of a material nature.</p>\n<p>Material</p>\n<p>A fact is material for the purposes of both non-disclosure and</p>\n<p>misrepresentation if it is one that would influence the judgment</p>\n<p>of a reasonable or prudent insurer in deciding whether or not to</p>\n<p>accept the risk or what premium to charge. In Container Transport</p>\n<p>International Inc. v. Oceanus Mutual Underwriting Association (Bermuda) Ltd</p>\n<p>(1984) 1 Lloyds Rep. 467, the court observed that the requirement</p>\n<p>that a fact must be one which would influence the judgment of a</p>\n<p>prudent insurer did not mean that an insurer must have acted</p>\n<p>differently if he had known the fact, but merely that he would</p>\n<p>have wanted to know of the fact when making his decision;</p>\n<p>“judgment” was construed as meaning “the formation of an</p>\n<p>opinion”, not the final decision.</p>\n<p>In general contract law, for a misrepresentation to be actionable,</p>\n<h4 id=\"there-must-be\">there must be:</h4>\n<p>• a statement of fact and not opinion or law;</p>\n<p>• it must be untrue or inaccurate;</p>\n<p>• it must be material to the making of the contract</p>\n<p>• it must be a statement of present fact and not as to the</p>\n<p>future</p>\n<p>• it must have induced the innocent party into making the</p>\n<p>insurance contract on the terms on which it was made</p>\n<h2 id=\"opinion\">OPINION</h2>\n<p>As mentioned above, the general rule is that a statement of</p>\n<p>opinion is not a fact. This is exemplified in the case of Bisset v</p>\n<p>Wilkinson [1927] AC 177. In this case, a farmer stated that “it was</p>\n<p>his opinion” that the land could hold 2,000 sheep. The plaintiff</p>\n<p>claimed for misrepresentation, but it was held not to be a</p>\n<p>statement of fact. This was due to two factors</p>\n<h3 id=\"1-he-expressly-stated-it-was-only-his-opinion\">1. He expressly stated it was only his opinion</h3>\n<h3 id=\"2-he-held-himself-out-as-having-no-expertise-as-to-whether-the\">2. He held himself out as having no expertise as to whether the</h3>\n<p>land held that many sheep, he had never claimed to keep sheep</p>\n<p>on the land, it was merely a guess. The plaintiff was also aware</p>\n<p>of this fact.</p>\n<p>Statements of law</p>\n<p>A statement of law which is incorrect will amount to a false statement of</p>\n<p>fact for the purpose of misrepresentation.</p>\n<p>Pankhania v LB Hackney [2002] EWHC 2441</p>\n<p>The claimants purchased property induced by a representation</p>\n<p>that the current occupiers of the property were contractual</p>\n<p>licensees, whose occupation could be terminated on giving 3</p>\n<p>months notice. In fact the current occupant was in fact a tenant</p>\n<p>protected under the Landlord and Tenant Act 1954. This was a</p>\n<p>misrepresentation as to law which had previously been assumed</p>\n<p>not to be an actionable misrepresentation through analogy with</p>\n<p>case law based on restitutionary claims for mistake of law. The</p>\n<p>rule barring</p>\n<p>recovery for mistake of law was abolished by the House of Lords</p>\n<p>in Kleinwort Benson v Lincoln County council. The High court</p>\n<p>held that actions based on misrepresentation of law could now be</p>\n<p>actionable based upon that change of law. The claimant&#x27;s action</p>\n<p>was therefore successful.</p>\n<p>False statement</p>\n<p>There must be a false statement of fact or law as oppose to</p>\n<p>opinion or estimate of future events</p>\n<p>Statements of intention</p>\n<p>A misrepresentation as to future intention is usually not actionable</p>\n<p>for misrepresentation, as it will not amount to a statement of fact.</p>\n<p>The statement of future intent will not be held to be a fact even if</p>\n<p>the defendant intentionally changes their mind as to their</p>\n<p>intentions (Inntrepreneur Pub Co v Sweeney [2002] EWHC 1060</p>\n<p>(Ch)).</p>\n<p>A statement of future intention made with absolutely no intention</p>\n<p>at the time of the statement, however, will amount to a</p>\n<p>misrepresentation, as seen in Edgington v Fitzmaurice(1885) 24 Ch</p>\n<h2 id=\"d-459\">D 459.</h2>\n<p>The representation must be material</p>\n<p>The representation must not be an inconsequential statement</p>\n<p>which is of irrelevance to the plaintiff. In order to be actionable,</p>\n<p>the representation must be material so that it would positively</p>\n<p>influence a reasonable person to enter the contract. In no case is it</p>\n<p>relevant to consider whether the non- disclosure would influence</p>\n<p>the particular insurer concerned or whether the assured himself</p>\n<p>thought that the facts were material. In JOEL v LAW</p>\n<p>UNION AND CROWN INSURANCE CO. Fletcher</p>\n<p>Moulton, L.J. stated: &quot;The disclosure must be of all you ought to</p>\n<p>have realised to be material, not of that only which you DID IN</p>\n<p>FACT realise to be so..... Your opinion of the materiality of that</p>\n<p>knowledge is of no moment. If a reasonable man would have</p>\n<p>recognised that it was material to dis. In the case of Container</p>\n<p>Transport          International Inc V. Oceanus Mutual</p>\n<p>Underwriting Association (Bermuda) Ltd (1984) 1 Lloyds</p>\n<p>Rep. 467, the court observed that the requirement that a fact</p>\n<p>must be one</p>\n<p>which would influence the judgment of a prudent insurer did not</p>\n<p>mean that an insurer must have acted differently if he had known</p>\n<p>the fact, but merely that he would have wanted to know of the</p>\n<p>fact when making his decision; “judgment” was construed as</p>\n<p>meaning “the formation of an opinion”, not the final decision.</p>\n<p>- Facts that need to be disclosed</p>\n<p>- Fraud</p>\n<h2 id=\"premium\">PREMIUM</h2>\n<h3 id=\"section-2-of-the-insurance-act-2017-is-on-interpretation-under-which\">Section 2 of the Insurance Act 2017 is on Interpretation under which</h3>\n<h4 id=\"the-following-are-given-meaning\">the following are given meaning:</h4>\n<p>An insurance contract is defined to be a contract under which one</p>\n<p>party, known as the insurer, in exchange for a premium, agrees</p>\n<p>with another party, known as the policy holder, to make a</p>\n<p>payment, or provide a benefit to the policy holder or another</p>\n<p>person on the occurrence of a specified uncertain event which, if</p>\n<p>it occurs, will be adverse to the interests of the policy holder or to</p>\n<p>the interests of the person who will receive the payment or</p>\n<p>benefit;</p>\n<p>Micro insurance is insurance for the protection of low-income</p>\n<p>people against specific perils in exchange for regular premium</p>\n<p>payments proportionate to the likelihood and cost of risk</p>\n<p>involved;</p>\n<p>Premium is the consideration for entering into an insurance</p>\n<p>contract;</p>\n<p>A licensee means a person who holds any licence issued under this</p>\n<p>Act.</p>\n<p>In insurance, consideration for assumption of risk is called premium,</p>\n<p>and it is governed by the Insurance Act.</p>\n<p>The law prior to the current position is that - one could have credits on</p>\n<p>premium;</p>\n<p>The current insurance act departs from the traditional English position.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-63-1\">Section 63(1):</h3>\n<p>(1) Subject to subsection (2), the insured shall pay in full the</p>\n<p>premiums payable under the insurance contract on or before the</p>\n<p>date of inception of the policy or renewal of the policy.</p>\n<p>(2) The Authority may by regulations provide for the payment of</p>\n<p>premiums in any other manner.</p>\n<p>- for there to be a valid insurance policy,you must pay the</p>\n<p>premium</p>\n<p>- you must renew the premium in full</p>\n<p>- for renewal of a policy, the premium must be paid in full.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-56-prohibits-the-unauthorized-provision-of-credit-on\">Section 56 prohibits the unauthorized provision of credit on</h3>\n<p>premiums.</p>\n<p>In Lewis Ltd v Norwich Union fire Insurance Co [1916] AC 509, 519 the</p>\n<p>court defined premium as “the consideration required of the insured for</p>\n<p>which the insurer undertook his obligation under the contract of</p>\n<p>insurance.” It was stated each party is supposed to fulfill its obligation</p>\n<p>under the contract and premium was the sum of money paid by the</p>\n<p>insured either as single lump sum or made in installments.</p>\n<p>What if one has signed a proposal form, and one signs a check: before the check clears,</p>\n<p>the event insured against occurs: is the insurer liable?</p>\n<p>The rules on premium are strict.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-importance-of\">THE             IMPORTANCE                      OF</h2>\n<h2 id=\"premium\">PREMIUM</h2>\n<p>In “The Importance of Insurance Premium Payment Moment, One of</p>\n<p>the Key Elements of Insurance Contract and the Respective</p>\n<p>Problematic” by Dr. Juliana Bylykbashi</p>\n<p>Insurance is the “delegation of responsibility for damage reward”. This</p>\n<p>means that the insurer through the insurance contract is obliged to</p>\n<p>indemnify the damage submitted by the insured for his person/or</p>\n<p>property. This insurance consists also to the return of an approximate</p>\n<p>damaged cash value.</p>\n<p>In material sense through civil legal relationship of insurance one party</p>\n<p>transfers to the other party the damage risk, which is the obligation for</p>\n<p>reward of a damage, in case it happens, despite this damage has not</p>\n<p>been caused because of the second party.</p>\n<p>The insurance contract is a term contract, which consists in time when</p>\n<p>this contract brings legal consequences, time when the insurance</p>\n<p>premium has to be paid to the insured and time when the insurance</p>\n<p>reward must be paid by the insurer.</p>\n<p>One party the insurer, if the circumstances provided by the contract are</p>\n<p>proved, which is the insurance case, undertakes to pay to the other</p>\n<p>party, to the insured or to a third person.</p>\n<p>It is clear that the insurer “…will undertake to pay to the other party</p>\n<p>…a reward in cash, only against a premium paid by the insured” This</p>\n<p>means that the premium payment is an essential condition of the</p>\n<p>insured which enables the execution of insurance contract by the</p>\n<p>insurer, so the cover that the last one will undertake to make to the</p>\n<p>insured in case of proving one of the insurance events.</p>\n<p>In McDonald V Law Union Insurance C.(1874)LR9 QB 328,</p>\n<p>Blackburn J stated that</p>\n<p>“Although the policy of insurance takes the form of unilateral</p>\n<p>undertaking by the insurers, to make the stipulated payment on the</p>\n<p>happening of the stipulated event, the contractual terms and conditions</p>\n<p>it contains are binding on the assured as they are on the insurers. Under</p>\n<p>Halsbury’s Laws of England,4th edition at page 243, the assured cannot</p>\n<p>therefore enforce the insurers promises as being contractual unless he in</p>\n<p>turn has performed any provisions which has to be performed by him</p>\n<p>to make the contract effective.”</p>\n<p>The issue of insurance premium is one of the main and significant</p>\n<p>points in insurance issues because paying the insurance premium</p>\n<p>corresponds exactly to the provision of insurer of the insured risk</p>\n<p>worth. The insurance premium is the risk price preptium periculi. Not</p>\n<p>paying the insurance premium, in some cases can bring the release of</p>\n<p>insurer from the obligation to cover the insurance in case the insured</p>\n<p>event is proved. This is also one of the main and the most important</p>\n<p>obligations of the policy holders.</p>\n<p>Failure to pay the premium or the first installment on the term entails</p>\n<p>the suspension of the insurance contract.</p>\n<p>In case the parties enter in this insurance contract, but the insured does</p>\n<p>not pay the premium, then we do not have a contract, for as long as</p>\n<p>there are no rights and obligations between them.</p>\n<p>We’re then able to conclude that the insurance contract is a consensual</p>\n<p>one, which enters in force after reaching the deal between the contract</p>\n<p>parties, without the need that one party gives or consign a certain</p>\n<p>object. So, the parties are considered to be in legal obligation</p>\n<p>relationships, enjoying the relevant and mutual rights and obligations.</p>\n<p>Considering that the main goal of insurance companies, as trade</p>\n<p>companies is profit realization, the paying of the premium is a condition</p>\n<p>sine qua non of existence of this contract.</p>\n<p>The insurance contract can never enter in force without payment of</p>\n<p>premium because the main condition to enter in the insurance contract</p>\n<p>is that of paying the premium. The moment of starting the responsibility</p>\n<p>of insurer is related to the payment of insurance premium, regardless of</p>\n<p>the fact that the contract is signed, the effects of the contract are</p>\n<p>conditionally or obligatorily related to premium payment.</p>\n<p>It holds the attitude that the property insurance contract is a consensual</p>\n<p>contract and though when the insurance event happened, the insured</p>\n<p>party has not fulfilled his own obligation (paying premium), for what</p>\n<p>above argued, the insurer, the defendant party has the obligation to pay</p>\n<p>to the plaintiff, the insured, the insurance reward within the limit of</p>\n<p>amount provided in the contract.</p>\n<p>Group Policies</p>\n<p>Does a claim provided after expiry of a policy amount to fraud?</p>\n<p>- MacGillivray</p>\n<p>- Longway Suitcase Manufacturers v UAP</p>\n<p>- Collinvaux on Insurance</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-64-price-control-mechanism-this-provision-is-on-approval-of\">Section 64 - Price control mechanism: this provision is on approval of</h3>\n<p>premium and commission rates – which must conform to the Insurance</p>\n<p>Regulatory Authority Rules.</p>\n<p>(1) An insurer or HMO shall not issue any policy of insurance if the</p>\n<p>premium rates and commission rates contravene any regulations</p>\n<p>made by the Authority under subsection (2).</p>\n<p>(2) The Authority may prescribe minimum premium or maximum</p>\n<p>commission rates for any class or type of insurance business.</p>\n<p>The present position in Uganda is that one cannot have a policy without</p>\n<p>paying premium in full.</p>\n<h2 id=\"insurable-interest\">INSURABLE INTEREST</h2>\n<h3 id=\"section-133-of-the-insurance-act-one-can-only-take-benefit-from-a\">Section 133 of the Insurance Act: One can only take benefit from a</h3>\n<p>policy either over a thing or a life in which one has an insurable interest.</p>\n<p>An insurable interest is essentially a possibility of loss. Only a person</p>\n<p>who stands to lose when the event insured against occurs can claim on a</p>\n<p>policy.</p>\n<p>There is an ambiguity under section 133(1) – where the first part of the</p>\n<p>provision covers ‘life’ and then the insurable interest relates to the ‘life</p>\n<p>or event.’ This is problematic because the potential beneficiary of the</p>\n<p>contract is not a party to the contract. The law has set out categories of</p>\n<p>people who can claim on a contract of life insurance – section 133(2).</p>\n<p>Subsections (a) to (c) are unqualified; age, success and ability have no</p>\n<p>bearing on whether a child or parent can take benefit from a life</p>\n<p>insurance benefit; subsections</p>\n<p>(d) and (f) are qualified because they relate to dependency. Also</p>\n<p>see section 5 of the Marine Insurance Act.</p>\n<p>People have a vested interest in the life insured if such life is</p>\n<p>dependent on them.</p>\n<p>-   Prudential Staff Union v. Hall (1925) K.B. 685</p>\n<h4 id=\"macaura-v-northern-assurance-1925-a-c-619\">-   Macaura v Northern Assurance (1925) A.C. 619:</h4>\n<p>-   Medical Defense Union v. Department of Trade [1979] 2 ALL ER 421</p>\n<p>-   Lucena v Crawford (1806) 2 Bos. &amp; Pul. 269 (professionals</p>\n<p>-     Mark Rowlands v Berni Inns (1985) 3 All ER</p>\n<p>-     C.I.C General Insurance Co. Ltd v Joseph Mwangi, Kenya HCCA 64/ 2015</p>\n<p>-     Beresford v Royal Insurance Co Ltd [1937] 2 All ER 243</p>\n<h4 id=\"note\">Note:</h4>\n<h3 id=\"1-insurable-interest-relates-to-how-directly-one-is-affected-by-the\">1. Insurable interest relates to how directly one is affected by the</h3>\n<p>occurrence of the event insured against in the insurance policy.</p>\n<h3 id=\"2-when-it-comes-to-life-the-question-of-an-insurable-interest\">2. When it comes to life, the question of an insurable interest</h3>\n<p>depends on the test of dependence and sustenance.</p>\n<h2 id=\"risk\">RISK</h2>\n<p>Risk is the thing one is protecting oneself from. It has to be very</p>\n<p>carefully defined because the insurer is only obliged to cover that thing</p>\n<p>which they understood to be their obligation to cover.</p>\n<p>Risk can neither be general nor vague.</p>\n<p>[Do a comparison between Global Process Systems Inc. v Syarikat Takaful</p>\n<p>Malaysia Bhd. (The Cendor MOPU) [2011] UKSC and The Financial Conduct</p>\n<p>Authority and Others v Arch Insurance (UK) Ltd and Others [2021] UKSC 1</p>\n<p>(15th January 2021)]</p>\n<p>Also compare: Café Chameleon CC v Guardrisk Insurance Company Ltd</p>\n<p>(5736/2020) [2020] ZAWCHC 65 and Ma-Afrika Hotels (Pty) Ltd and</p>\n<p>Another v Santam Limited (6499/2020) [2020] ZAWCHC 160; [2021] 1</p>\n<p>All SA 195 (WCC)</p>\n<p>-  Issue in the Cendor MOPU Whether the stress affecting the rig</p>\n<p>while it was being transported was part of the risk covered by the</p>\n<p>insurance rig.</p>\n<p>- The Financial Conduct Authority involves a distinction between</p>\n<p>business interruption and force majeure.</p>\n<p>Courts typically interpret risk in a restrictive manner – and the risk must</p>\n<p>be understood by both parties.</p>\n<p>Read McGillivray on Risk and compare with Collinvaux.</p>\n<h2 id=\"claims\">CLAIMS</h2>\n<p>The insured has a duty to notify the insurer immediately once the risk</p>\n<p>occur.</p>\n<p>The insured must notify within the terms and requirements of the</p>\n<p>policy.</p>\n<p>-   MicroCare v Centenary Rural Development Bank, Miscellaneous Cause No.</p>\n<p>329 of 2017;</p>\n<p>One must never make a false claim, lest it is deemed fraudulent: this</p>\n<p>arises from the principle of utmost good faity – the claim has to be</p>\n<p>honest and accurate.</p>\n<p>Does this mean that every falsehood amounts to fraud?</p>\n<p>o Read Longway Suitcase Manufacturing Co. v UAP Insurance (U) Ltd [2014]</p>\n<h2 id=\"ugcommc-146\">UGCOMMC 146</h2>\n<p>Plaintiff’s action for 1.83 million US Dollars, general damages,</p>\n<p>interest and costs of the suit. They claimed under a policy of</p>\n<p>insurance from May 2010 – in consideration of premium paid against</p>\n<p>loss or damage by fire. Property at the factory was destroyed by fire</p>\n<p>on 16th July, 2010. It was common ground that the insurance policy</p>\n<p>was valid at the time the risk occurred.</p>\n<p>The defendant denied that the plaintiff had a claim. Further, they</p>\n<p>argued that the fire was self-inflicted and amounted to arson on the</p>\n<p>plaintiff’s part. The fire was not accidental. Secondly, the defendant</p>\n<p>argued that Longway did not comply with the terms of the policy.</p>\n<p>Alternatively, they submitted that the plaintiff exaggerated the</p>\n<p>damages, and the claim was not a true and fair reflection of the</p>\n<p>property that was destroyed in the fire.</p>\n<p>On arson</p>\n<p>The plaintiff argued that in cases of an insurance policy, the</p>\n<p>defendant must prove the assertion that the plaintiff is guilty of</p>\n<p>arson – Slattery v Mance [1962] 1 All ER 525. This burden must be</p>\n<p>discharged on a balance of probabilities. However where the jury</p>\n<p>comes to the conclusion that the loss is equally consistent with</p>\n<p>arson as it is with an accidental fire, the onus being on the</p>\n<p>Defendant, the Plaintiff would win on that issue.</p>\n<p>It was observed that the defendant ‘was doing everything possible</p>\n<p>to keep the findings that were not favorable to its</p>\n<p>position away from the plaintiff.” This demonstrated “bad faith and</p>\n<p>dirty hands” of the defendant.</p>\n<p>There was nothing concrete to support arson on the part of the</p>\n<p>plaintiff.</p>\n<p>On breach of the policy</p>\n<p>The defendant argued that the plaintiff did not comply with terms of</p>\n<p>the policy, especially clause 033 which required fire extinguishers on</p>\n<p>the premises.</p>\n<p>The plaintiff contended that in light of evidence on record, the</p>\n<p>defendant waived the provision for fire extinguishers: they never</p>\n<p>explained to the plaintiff that they had to have firefighting equipment</p>\n<p>on the premises – and they had confirmed, upon inspection of the</p>\n<p>premises – that everything was okay.</p>\n<p>Estoppels in the context of insurance are discussed in Motor Oil Hellas</p>\n<p>(Corinth) Refineries SA v Shipping Corporation Of India, Kanchenjunga (1990)1</p>\n<h4 id=\"lloyd-s-rep-391\">Lloyd’s Rep 391:</h4>\n<p>&quot;Equitable estoppels occurs where a person, having legal rights</p>\n<p>against another, unequivocally represents (by words or</p>\n<p>conduct) that he does not intend to enforce those legal rights; if</p>\n<p>in such circumstances the other party acts, or desist from</p>\n<p>acting, in reliance upon that representation, with the effect that</p>\n<p>it would be inequitable for the representor thereafter to enforce</p>\n<p>his legal rights inconsistently with his representation, he will to</p>\n<p>that extent to be precluded from doing so.&quot;</p>\n<p>On market value of the goods</p>\n<p>Court held that prima facie, the value of the property is measured</p>\n<p>on the basis of market value – Halsbury’s Laws of England, 4th Ed.</p>\n<p>2003 Reissue Vol. 25 (Insurance) at para 629.</p>\n<p>On fraud as alleged by the defendant</p>\n<p>The particulars of fraud were not pleaded: in Haji Abdul Nasser</p>\n<p>Katende versus Vithaldas Haridas &amp; Co. Ltd Civil Appeal No 84 of 2003 it</p>\n<p>was held that the standard of proof for fraud is higher than the</p>\n<p>balance of probabilities in civil cases. However the standard of proof</p>\n<p>is not so high as to require proof beyond reasonable doubt.</p>\n<p>Court’s Judgment</p>\n<p>Court found that “an acceptance of an insurance policy will be of no</p>\n<p>effect in law unless the parties have agreed to every material term</p>\n<p>of the contract they wish to make.” – see McGillivray on Insurance at</p>\n<p>99.</p>\n<p>The amount and subject matter of insurance are fundamental terms</p>\n<p>which have to be agreed upon.</p>\n<p>PW2 – the person responsible for obtaining the insurance policy</p>\n<p>from the defendant on behalf of the plaintiff demonstrate that he did</p>\n<p>not understand the subject matter of insurance – and during the period</p>\n<p>of signing the policy – he had signed 6 contracts in Chinese for the</p>\n<p>manufacture or assembly of suitcases on commission. Each stipulated</p>\n<p>that the plaintiff was responsible for loss by fire or theft. In thiscase,</p>\n<p>there was        no        valid contract ever               executed</p>\n<p>between the parties.</p>\n<p>On circumstantial evidence to prove arson</p>\n<p>Teper v Reginam [1952] 2 All ER 447 – it was held that circumstantial</p>\n<p>evidence must always be narrowly examined because it may be</p>\n<p>fabricated to cast suspicion on another.</p>\n<p>At 451: “The circumstantial evidence falls short of conclusiveness and a properly</p>\n<p>instructed jury, having it alone before it, would have had a more than usually difficult</p>\n<p>decision to make.” Hearsay evidence is inadmissible unless it forms part of</p>\n<p>res gestae.</p>\n<p>On fraud</p>\n<p>Fraud must be strictly proved – Kampala Bottlers Limited v Damanico (U)</p>\n<p>Limited, Civil Appeal 22/1992. It is ‘actual dishonesty on the part of the</p>\n<p>person alleged to have acted fraudulently, which dishonesty deprives or</p>\n<p>is calculated to deprive the victim of something.</p>\n<h4 id=\"on-claims\">On claims:</h4>\n<p>Claims may be unenforceable on the grounds of public policy.</p>\n<p>Geismar v Sun Alliance and London Insurance Ltd and another</p>\n<p>[1977] 3 All ER 570, the Plaintiff insured against theft and while the policies</p>\n<p>were in force various articles were stolen from his house. This included some</p>\n<p>property which had been imported into the United Kingdom by the Plaintiff</p>\n<p>without being declared to customs and excise officers and without payment of</p>\n<p>the required duty. Talbot J held at page 580 – 581 that the policy would be</p>\n<p>unenforceable in respect of the smuggled articles and to enforce them would</p>\n<h4 id=\"be-in-conflict-with-the-public-policy\">be in conflict with the public policy:</h4>\n<p>“It seems to me that .... These smuggled articles are in the same category as</p>\n<p>the forbidden cargo in Parkin v Dick. No new area of public policy is involved</p>\n<p>here. The Plaintiff is seeking the assistance of the court to enforce contracts of</p>\n<p>insurance so that he may be indemnified against loss of articles which he</p>\n<p>deliberately and intentionally imported into this country in breach of the</p>\n<p>Customs and Excise Act 1952.</p>\n<p>... But where there is a deliberate breach of the law I do not think the court</p>\n<p>ought to assist the Plaintiff to derive a profit from it, even though it is sought</p>\n<p>indirectly through an indemnity under an insurance policy.”</p>\n<p>In Mackender and Others v Feldia A G and Others [1966] 3 All ER</p>\n<h4 id=\"847-lord-denning-mr-at-850-held-that\">847 Lord Denning MR at 850 held that:</h4>\n<p>“As to illegality, I would only say this. The underwriters were clearly innocent.</p>\n<p>The diamond merchants may have had an unlawful intention to smuggle goods</p>\n<p>into a friendly foreign country; but their illegality would not affect the formation</p>\n<p>of the contract. It would only make it unenforceable. It would mean that they</p>\n<p>could not recover on the policy.”</p>\n<p>The law is that goods which were not declared to the Revenue</p>\n<p>Authority for purposes of payment of customs dues cannot be included</p>\n<p>in a claim for indemnity for their loss. However the burden is on the</p>\n<p>Defendant to prove that the Plaintiff did not have the quantity of goods</p>\n<p>for which indemnification is sought. Secondly that the origin of the</p>\n<p>goods is outside the country and ought to have been declared to the</p>\n<p>customs authorities. As to the quantity of the goods affected by the</p>\n<p>fire the only acceptable evidence is that of the Defendant’s officials</p>\n<p>who visited the site of the fire as well as the Plaintiff&#x27;s witnesses.</p>\n<p>On the principle of good faith</p>\n<p>The principle of utmost good faith is a fundamental principle of insurance law. It is a contract</p>\n<p>“uberrimae fidei” (of utmost good faith). According to Osborn&#x27;s Concise Law Dictionary</p>\n<p>(supra) at page 421 the principle connotes the duty of a promisee to communicate to the</p>\n<p>promisor every fact and circumstance which may influence him in deciding to enter into the</p>\n<p>contract or not. &quot;Contracts of insurance of every kind are of this class.</p>\n<p>Court went on to find that determining the quantum of loss is a question of fact.</p>\n<p>Its duty is to ascertain from the available evidence whether the plaintiff had the</p>\n<p>quantity of goods claimed for – and the next question is whether the goods are</p>\n<p>valued at an acceptable rate or overpriced: “overpricing is not evidence of</p>\n<p>fraud”. Lastly, the question is whether all goods have been imported for</p>\n<p>purposes of ascertaining the quantity and value of the goods before making a</p>\n<p>conclusion on whether there was a fraudulent claim by the plaintiff.</p>\n<p>Read Collinvaux and McGillivray on Claims.</p>\n<p>A man may insure against loss caused by the illegal acts of his</p>\n<p>employees or Defendants. It would be no objection to his recovery</p>\n<p>under fire policy to show that the servant or probably even his wife had</p>\n<p>wilfully burned the premises, provided that the insured himself was not</p>\n<p>privy to the act – paras 356</p>\n<p>The standard of proof is not proof beyond that of a reasonable doubt.</p>\n<p>It is proof on the balance of probabilities that the insured wilfully</p>\n<p>caused the fire – at 482 &amp; 483.</p>\n<p>There are certain fundamentals in an insurance contract which must be</p>\n<p>agreed upon between the insurers and the assured. An acceptance of an</p>\n<p>insurance policy will be of no effect in law unless the parties have</p>\n<p>agreed upon every material term of the contract they wish to make. The</p>\n<h4 id=\"material-terms-of-the-insurance-are-discussed-at-page-99-to-include\">material terms of the insurance are discussed at page 99 to include:</h4>\n<p>&quot;the definition of the risk to be covered, the duration of the</p>\n<p>insurance cover, the amount and mode of payment of the premium</p>\n<p>and the amount of the insurance payable in the event of loss. As to</p>\n<p>all these there must be a consensus ad idem, that is to say, there must</p>\n<p>either be an express agreement or the circumstances must be such as</p>\n<p>to admit of a reasonable inference that the parties were tacitly</p>\n<p>agreed. Without such agreement, it would be impossible for the</p>\n<p>courts to give effect to the parties’ contract except by virtually writing</p>\n<p>the contract for them, which is not the function of the court to do.&quot;</p>\n<p>According to MacGillivray on Insurance Law (supra) at page 197</p>\n<p>paragraph 8 – 6 , if premiums have been paid to the insurer with an</p>\n<p>application for insurance, but no binding contract of insurance is in fact</p>\n<p>concluded, the money is recoverable as paid for a consideration</p>\n<p>which has wholly failed. That is the situation here and the Plaintiff is</p>\n<p>entitled to a refund of the premium paid.</p>\n<p>- Lickiss v Milestone Motor Policies (1966) 2 All E.R 972 (on breach – and</p>\n<p>waiver - of condition in an insurance contract)</p>\n<p>A motor cyclist was insured under a motor policy against third</p>\n<p>party risks. Condition 1 of the policy provided that “the insured</p>\n<p>shall give full particulars in writing” to the insurers “as soon as</p>\n<p>possible after the occurrence of any accident … and shall forward</p>\n<p>immediately any letter, notice of intended prosecution, writ,</p>\n<p>summons or process relating thereto“. That condition was a</p>\n<p>condition precedent to any liability of the insurers. On 17 May</p>\n<p>1964, the motor cyclist collided with a taxicab, which was</p>\n<p>damaged. The motor cyclist received a notice of intended</p>\n<p>prosecution, and was served with a summons returnable on 2 July</p>\n<p>1964, By letter of 18 June 1964, the police informed the insurers of</p>\n<p>these proceedings. On 23 June 1964, the insurers wrote to the motor</p>\n<p>cyclist—“We understand that proceedings are being taken against</p>\n<p>you on July 2 … It would be appreciated if you would let us know</p>\n<p>why you have not notified us of these proceedings, since we will wish</p>\n<p>to arrange your defence. We await your comments“. On 2 July 1964,</p>\n<p>the motor cyclist pleaded guilty by letter and was fined.</p>\n<p>£5. On 10 August 1964, the insurers repudiated liability. In an</p>\n<p>action by the taxicab proprietor the motor cyclist was held liable for</p>\n<p>the damage to the taxicab, and failed to recover indemnity as</p>\n<p>against the insurers on the ground of his breach of condition 1 by</p>\n<p>not having sent to them the notice of prosecution and the</p>\n<p>summons.</p>\n<p>Held – the motor cyclist’s claim was not barred by condition 1 of the</p>\n<p>policy, but he was entitled to recover from the insurers in respect of</p>\n<p>his liability to the taxicab proprietor, for the following reasons—</p>\n<p>(i)    the insurers’ letter of 23 June 1964, was tantamount to their</p>\n<p>saying that they did not want to see the notice of prosecution</p>\n<p>and the summons, and the sending of the letter in the</p>\n<p>circumstances was a waiver of the terms of condition 1</p>\n<p>regarding the notice and summons; accordingly the defence</p>\n<p>founded on breach of condition 1 failed, and the motor</p>\n<p>cyclist was entitled to recover on the policy in respect of his</p>\n<p>liability to the taxicab proprietor (see p 976, letters a c and f,</p>\n<p>post). Plasticmoda Societa per Azioni v Davidsons (Manchester) Ltd</p>\n<p>([1952] 1 Lloyds’ Rep 527) applied.</p>\n<p>(ii)     (per Lord Denning MR and Danckwerts LJ; Salmon LJ</p>\n<p>dissenting) since the insurers had received all the necessary</p>\n<p>information about the prosecution from the police, it was</p>\n<p>unnecessary for the motor cyclist to give the insurers the same</p>\n<p>information under condition 1 of the policy, and the insurers</p>\n<p>could no longer rely on that condition (see p 976, letters b and</p>\n<p>c, post).</p>\n<p>Appeal allowed.</p>\n<p>-</p>\n<p>-   Beresford v Royal Insurance [1999] Lloyd&#x27;s Rep IR 29 Beresford v</p>\n<p>Royal Insurance is a case heard by the English High Court.</p>\n<p>The     case concerned             a dispute between a</p>\n<p>policyholder, Mr. Beresford, and his insurer, Royal Insurance,</p>\n<p>over the policyholder&#x27;s claim for the loss of use of his vehicle</p>\n<p>following an accident.</p>\n<p>Facts: Mr. Beresford was involved in an accident which rendered</p>\n<p>his vehicle un-roadworthy but did not cause any physical damage</p>\n<p>to the vehicle. The policyholder made a claim to his insurer, Royal</p>\n<p>Insurance, for the loss of use of his vehicle. The insurer rejected</p>\n<p>the claim on the grounds that the policy only provided coverage</p>\n<p>for physical damage to the vehicle and not for the loss of use.</p>\n<p>Holding: The court held that the insurer was liable to pay</p>\n<p>compensation to the policyholder for the loss of use of his</p>\n<p>vehicle, even though the vehicle was not physically damaged in</p>\n<p>the accident. This ruling established that the right to receive</p>\n<p>compensation for loss of use is a separate and distinct right from</p>\n<p>the right to receive compensation for physical damage to the</p>\n<p>vehicle.</p>\n<p>-   Suffish    International    Foods    Processors   Uganda   Ltd   and</p>\n<p>another v Egypt Air Corporation [2003] 1 EA 330 (SCU)</p>\n<p>-   Lord Napier and Ettrick &amp; Anor v Hunter and others ; Lord Napier and</p>\n<p>Ettrick v R F Kershaw Ltd and others [1993] 1 All ER 385</p>\n<p>-</p>\n<p>-   Mawadri v Britt Syndicates &amp; Anor HCCS 376/2009</p>\n<p>-   Galloway v. Royal Guardian Royal Exchange (UK) ltd (1999) Lloyd’s</p>\n<p>Rep. I.R. 209</p>\n<h2 id=\"next-lecture\">NEXT LECTURE</h2>\n<h2 id=\"1-reinstatement\">1. REINSTATEMENT</h2>\n<h2 id=\"2-conditions-and-warranties\">2. CONDITIONS AND WARRANTIES</h2>\n<p>a. It must be exactly complied with</p>\n<p>In the case of Marine Insurance Co. v. Ralli Bros. (1913), the</p>\n<p>court held that a warranty must be strictly complied with</p>\n<p>and that a breach of warranty discharges the insurer&#x27;s</p>\n<p>liability, even if the breach is minor or accidental.</p>\n<p>b. A warranty need not be material to the risk</p>\n<p>c. There is no remedy for breach</p>\n<p>d. There is no defence to the breach</p>\n<p>e. It is a promissory condition precedent</p>\n<p>“In policies of marine insurance, I think it is settled by authority</p>\n<p>that any statement of a fact bearing upon the risk introduced into</p>\n<p>the written policy is, by whatever words and in whatever place, to</p>\n<p>be construed as a warranty, and, prima facie, at least that the</p>\n<p>compliance with the warranty is a condition precedent to the</p>\n<p>attaching of the risk… if a promissory warranty is not complied</p>\n<p>with, the insurer is discharged from liability as from the date of</p>\n<p>the breach of warranty, for the simple reason that fulfilment of</p>\n<p>the warranty is a condition precedent to the liability or further</p>\n<p>liability of the insurer. This, moreover, reflects the fact that the</p>\n<p>rationale of warranties in insurance law is that the insurer only</p>\n<p>accepts the risk provided that the warranty is fulfilled.”</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-causation\">3. CAUSATION</h2>\n<h3 id=\"section-55-1-of-the-marine-insurance-act-1906-encapsulates-the\">Section 55(1) of the Marine Insurance Act 1906 encapsulates the</h3>\n<p>general rule of causation to be applied for the purpose of resolving</p>\n<h4 id=\"disputes-regarding-the-cause-of-loss-it-states\">disputes regarding the cause of loss. It states:</h4>\n<p>Subject to the provisions of this Act, and unless the policy</p>\n<p>otherwise provides, the insurer is liable for any loss</p>\n<p>proximately caused by a peril insured against, but, subject as</p>\n<p>aforesaid, he is not liable for any loss which is not</p>\n<p>proximately caused by a peril insured against.</p>\n<p>It declares the principle that the liability of the insurer hinges upon</p>\n<p>the loss or damage being ‘proximately’ caused by a peril insured</p>\n<p>against. The general rule of causa proxima, non remota, spectatur</p>\n<p>is well established in insurance law, and unless this is complied</p>\n<p>with, the insurer bears no liability. It is, therefore, important that</p>\n<p>the meaning of the word ‘proximate’ be fully understood as it</p>\n<p>applies to marine insurance.</p>\n<p>Though causa proxima, non remota, spectatur is the general principle to</p>\n<p>be observed, nevertheless, the opening words of the section allow</p>\n<p>exceptions to the rule. The phrase ‘subject to the provisions of the</p>\n<p>Act’ warns that the Act itself may depart from the maxim. This is</p>\n<p>evident, for example, in ss 39(5) and 55(2)(a), where the term</p>\n<p>‘attributable to’ is employed. With regard to s 39(5), any loss</p>\n<p>‘attributable to’ such unseaworthiness to which the assured is</p>\n<p>privy is not recoverable. Similarly, any loss or damage ‘attributable</p>\n<p>to’ the wilful misconduct of the assured is not recoverable under s</p>\n<p>55(2)(a)</p>\n<p>.</p>\n<p>In Leyland Shipping Company Limited v Norwich Union Fire Insurance</p>\n<h4 id=\"1918-ac-350-the-concept-of-causation-is-explained-thus\">[1918] AC 350, the concept of causation is explained thus:</h4>\n<p>In this case, a ship – the Ikaria - was insured against (amongst</p>\n<p>other things) perils of the sea by a time policy containing a</p>\n<p>warranty against all consequences of hostilities. The ship, while on</p>\n<p>a voyage from South America to Havre, was torpedoed by a</p>\n<p>German submarine twenty-five miles from Havre. The torpedo</p>\n<p>struck her well forward and she began to settle down by the head,</p>\n<p>but with the aid of tugs she reached Havre on the evening of the</p>\n<p>same day, when she was taken alongside a quay in the outer</p>\n<p>harbour. A gale sprang up, causing her to bump against the quay,</p>\n<p>and the harbour authorities fearing that she would sink and block</p>\n<p>the quay, ordered her to a berth inside the outer breakwater,</p>\n<p>where she was moored. She remained there for two days, taking</p>\n<p>the ground at each ebb tide, but floating again with the flood, and</p>\n<p>finally her bulkheads gave way, and she sank and became a total</p>\n<p>loss.</p>\n<p>The ship-owners brought an action on the policy claiming to</p>\n<p>recover as for a loss by perils of the sea:-</p>\n<p>Court held that the grounding was not a novus causa interveniens, and</p>\n<p>that the torpedoing was the proximate cause of the loss; and</p>\n<p>consequently – the underwriters were protected by the warranty</p>\n<p>against all consequences of hostilities.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-the-effect-of-fraud-on-an-insurance-contract\">4. THE EFFECT OF FRAUD ON AN INSURANCE CONTRACT</h2>\n<h3 id=\"5-a-comparison-between-global-process-systems-inc-v-syarikat-takaful\">5. A comparison between Global Process Systems Inc. v Syarikat Takaful</h3>\n<p>Malaysia Bhd. (The Cendor MOPU) [2011] UKSC and The Financial</p>\n<p>Conduct Authority and Others v Arch Insurance (UK) Ltd and Others</p>\n<p>[2021] UKSC 1 (15th January 2021</p>\n<h3 id=\"6-with-the-aid-of-decided-cases-the-principles-of-prudential\">6. “With the aid of decided cases, the principles of prudential</h3>\n<p>management of Health Insurers… (question incomplete)</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-role-of-the-regulator\">7. ROLE OF THE REGULATOR</h2>\n<h2 id=\"8-the-principles-governing-insolvency-of\">8. THE      PRINCIPLES        GOVERNING INSOLVENCY OF</h2>\n<p>INSURANCE COMPANIES (whether those principles have</p>\n<p>evolved in the wake of the COVID-19 pandemic)</p>\n<h3 id=\"9-the-law-policy-and-principles-on-intermediaries-in-uganda\">9. The Law, Policy and Principles on Intermediaries in Uganda</h3>\n<p>and whether it is in tandem with common law principles.</p>";
